window.FORAS_ALBUMS=[{"id":"album-01","artist":"Spectral Vision","title":"Through Ancient Mists","genre":"Dungeon Synth","date":"2026-10-18","cover":"img/album-01/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-10-18T19:03:29+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepY9bUkQux/","text":"1. Through Ancient Mists. 10:40\n2. The Forgotten Keep. 08:32\n3. Echoes of the Elder Kingdom. 04:37\n4. Stairway Beyond the Mist. 05:07\n5. The Last Watch. 09:21\nTotal Running Time:\n\nAs bitter winds continue to sweep across dear old Blighty, we’re now almost at the end of October and it would appear that the weather has finally lost its bizarre complex of giving two different seasons in a month; yes the random scorching afternoons are a thing of the past; we now have cold mornings and evenings. So there’s no wonder I ended up plagued with illness last week much like the rest of the population. Of course, the wretched Covid word has seemed to have raised its ugly head and could well be striking fear into the population once again; me not so much, I’ll stay here high in my hollow away from it all and people too. Which brings me back to last week in a roundabout way. Cast your minds back to yet another postal dilemma I faced at the hands of Royal Mail’s total incompetence and lack of reading skills. So, whilst in my hollow last week I received two notifications simultaneously that two parcels had been delivered, yet when I reached my hollow’s entrance the postie handed me just the one; after scouring the postie’s soul for information, I got nothing. Thankfully my dear dungeon brother had all the details and the item was retrieved and reviewed last week, not to mention giving crippling fear to the old gentleman I retrieved it from, to whom I apologise; he thought I’d come to collect more than the parcel in question, but what about the one I received at my hollow’s entrance I hear you ask? Well, that is where today’s review comes into play. Sitting here at the edge of my hollow the steam from my hot cup of the black stuff gently entwining with the smoke from my cigarette as they flow softly out on yet another chilly misty morning I sit looking through the sleeve of my delivery from last week; that’s right in my rotting hands I hold a copy of Spectral Visions “Through Ancient Mists.”","img":["img/album-01/p01-1.webp","img/album-01/p01-2.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-10-18T19:05:06+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepZJOokW7s/","text":"Yes, after hearing less than a few seconds of this I had to have it; not only that I had to review it. Sadly my schedule has been a little hectic and this has been out since the beginning of September still; with this month being what it is, what better time than to be reviewing within what’s now come to be collectively known as Spooky Season, or just October for me; every day is Halloween; I mean have you seen what I look like? \n\tAnyway, moving on, it would appear that this is not the only release by this entity; no, there are three prior releases with another having just recently dropped, and with this and a few little titbits of information gleaned from the wonderful web I pick myself up and wander to my slope. Yes, usually I’d be pouring over my map and plotting a course to go and visit the country of origin of this entity; however, this entity is quite the mystery and not wanting to disturb or bring any unwanted attention to his hometown I opt to stay here in my hollow, after all the the torches are burning bright in their sconces and that chill that sweeps down the hollow tunnel of my hollow is making me feel right at home. So, I prepare to delve deep into this review; my station is prepped, new parchment, fresh ink and coffee (not to confuse the two) and an army of cigarettes rolled I feel prepared for the journey I’m about to undertake, with my crusty orange headphones slid over my pitted rough skull I ready myself to press play on my tired old Walkman.","img":["img/album-01/p02-2.webp"]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-10-18T19:10:47+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepZy8xkRWy/","text":"Track one “Through Ancient Mists” starts with some deep and undulating chords and the higher notes simultaneously. Light crackling can be heard in the background and it feels like you’re listening to this on an old vinyl. The sound is rich and very crisp even given the odd crackling you hear, I even try testing the connection of my headphones just to make sure these battered old things aren’t failing me, but alas it is indeed the added production sound. Sitting at my slope the music fills my skull and a thick mist starts to enter my hollow, struggling to even focus on my parchment as I pen this even my candles grow dim as do the torches on my wall, I stand grabbing a burning torch and walking to see where this is emanating from, reaching my hollow’s entrance things seem to change and shift, stepping out of my hollow, an ancient path lies before me. The music still filling my skull with its wonderful slightly mournful tones I float down this old path that disappears into the thick heavy mist. As the music progresses you get little wavering moments from the synth that whilst they don’t seem intentional they actually match the movement of the mist that’s before me. Little undulations in its formation match these little wavering moments and I slowly float down this seemingly endless path. Torch in one rotting hand I wonder just what will lie in wait for me on this journey.","img":["img/album-01/p03-2.webp"]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-10-18T19:13:16+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepaFGGEQZx/","text":"This is classic dungeon synth and whilst quite modern in the time that this has been issued it has all the tropes and markings of a classic piece. Digging in a makeshift pocket of my robes I pull out the cassette; its stunningly thick J-card, hand-cut and printed feels old to the touch. This has been produced in the finest and truest way possible and matches the aesthetic of the music perfectly. Placing it safely in my pocket I continue down the path, the torch barely lighting my way through the thick fog. The track shifts and gently changes at just over the halfway point; even the synth’s tone slightly changes, matching the atmosphere I stand in, the sun has indeed set and night has drawn in; the synth’s keys mimic insects you’d hear on a cool evening walk. Just where are we heading? And will I glimpse anyone on my journey? Alone with this wonderful atmospheric music I drift on further; even the notes sound like the mist that stands before me, they have a slight hiss to them as well as an echoing type quality like they’re bouncing back off the wall of grey that stands before me, almost giving an ethereal quality, like if the mist was indeed slightly spectral in a sense. Maybe it is; maybe this mist is guiding me to something bigger than one could imagine. It’s all-encompassing, and the sound truly engulfs you with its rich and thick sound I’m very in awe of all of this and I love the little flourishes of days past that it gives. It’s like you dug this release out of a dungeon synth time capsule of the 90’s.","img":["img/album-01/p04-2.webp"]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-10-18T19:15:07+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepaSmpkVHb/","text":"All the hallmarks are there and I’m just overwhelmed by the dedication to it; however, my journey must carry on. This over ten-minute track keeps me company for the duration of my journey and now I’m here at the shoreline, waves lapping against the wet and jagged rocks. I can make out something through the mist and spray from the waves; however, unsure what I glimpse I decide to venture further. That’s not before I wake up at my slope. Headphones still on my skull coffee trickling down my parchment and an unlit cigarette dangling from where one of my teeth once resided. I look at the parchment and see just what I’d penned, where had I been and for how long? I slam the coffee and prepare another cup; maybe I drifted off maybe this illness hasn’t totally left my presence either way I was deep in the mix and this music has the power to indeed do that.","img":["img/album-01/p05-2.webp"]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-10-18T19:17:23+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepajLuEWyD/","text":"Track two “The Forgotten Keep” starts slightly similar to that of its predecessor; here though those thick undulating chords have since been changed to ones that are more longer and drawn out, while the lighter keys both working in complete unison give off a much lighter and airing feeling, a sense of fatigue washes over me once more and I’m back stood at the shore line once more. The mist is starting to shift and high up on a cliff an old fortress can be seen. This fortress is tired and crumbling, sections of its turrets lost to battles and time. This is the silent witness to a kingdom erased from memory. The moonlight pierces through its missing sections bathing it in an almost ethereal glow and it’s here that the music matches that very vision; the synth adopts a more piano tone, or the use of a piano is played alongside the synth; either way, it almost sounds like the music that would’ve been played inside the great hall of the keep that stands before me. It also has a slightly haunting sound; the piano has an echoing quality to it like it still plays out into this cold still air. Then suddenly it stops and we’re only left with the soft tones of the synth. Again there are slight wavers and crackles to this track too giving that old sound once again and matching it to that of the track before.","img":["img/album-01/p06-2.webp"]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-10-18T19:19:05+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepavtsEX1_/","text":"Floating closer and closer the fortress becomes much more imposing, its walls tall and thick; the one turret still in one piece while the remaining two are in despair; they’re just mere fragments of what they once were, this fortress looks less purposely built and more as if it emerged from the cliff itself, giving it a more organic look and feel. The keys of the piano resurface at the halfway point and as I float around the fortress they act like haunting tomes of what was once played here many years ago. Their sound truly echoes from these hollow stone walls. Placing my hand upon its cold stone walls the sound of the piano’s wavering and faltering notes makes even me tremble. I feel very at home here outside this keep and its power and presence are held within the notes of this track. Whilst looking tired bedraggled and unloved this place was once home to a noble man, one of great significance and power. You truly do get the sense from every note played and it resonates both in my skull and from every stone. The track fades slowly and so does the keep from my steely gaze, coming to rest once again at my slope, with no recollection of ever having left my hollow. Yet my parchment tells me of such; this is truly an impressive and immersive two tracks so far and there is still more to come.","img":["img/album-01/p07-2.webp"]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-10-18T19:22:17+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepbHHQEcfZ/","text":"Track three “Echoes of the Elder Kingdom” opens with soft chords and piano keys once more; as soon as the crackling and wavering of the notes appear I fall off into a place I have no idea where it may be; however, I’m here and the music still follows me. I’m now heading inside the keep and as I do the beautiful ethereal sounds of the album follow. I run my hands down the crumbling and weathered stone walls, and the echoes of not just the keys from the track can be felt but from the lineage that was there many years ago. Visions of crowned rulers, abandoned keeps and lost realms all come flooding back and the music that accompanies me matches that from the regal piano sound that resembles lost crowned royalty to the wavering synth tones that bring visions of other abandoned keeps and lost realms. This is what I love about these wavering synth moments they make these moments really stand out; they make the transition of visions stunning and the shift between them seamless. Now this is a short track which makes my time within these stone walls sadly short also. Tired and very weary from all that I have envisioned the music slowly fades as does the feeling I’ve been on yet another part of this incredible journey. I’m back in my hollow once more and my parchment is slowly filling up. Taking another sip of coffee to ensure it’s not tiredness luring me off to such places I also light up a cigarette, inhaling deeply; the smoke billows out from where my mandible would usually reside and the next track starts.","img":["img/album-01/p08-2.webp"]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-10-18T19:27:43+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Depbu69kVJ3/","text":"Track four “Stairway Beyond the Mist” opens with some truly ominous chords and much deeper notes than those of the previous tracks. This is a much darker sound, and as I come to once again there’s a reason why. Upon leaving the Keep I am faced with an ancient staircase, where where this leads is unclear as the stairs descend into pitch black. Drawing deep on my cigarette, I see the smoke plume down the stairs; it ripples and cascades down like a never-ending waterfall. I rummage in the pocket of my robes and pull out a lighter, striking it I slowly ascend down the stairs where to I shall never know. The music whilst not as dark and eerie as some artists would’ve chosen to have used it does indeed have a much darker element to it. Played at the lower end of the synth these chords almost billow and echo like they’re emerging from the darkness below. Travelling further and further below the surface light refuses to exist and darkness envelops me completely. This I am truly at home with as darkness and my feathery friend is all that keeps me company. This track heavily reminds me of my time spent with the bards of Gilgareth and it wouldn’t surprise me if this wasn’t some form of influence as on their darker tracks the sound that is here on this release does indeed bear similar tones and components found here, also down to them both hailing from Germany. However, I have been known for being wrong but this could possibly be a subtle nod to say the least.","img":["img/album-01/p09-2.webp"]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-10-18T19:29:44+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Depb9vxETFJ/","text":"However, back to this staircase, it would appear to lead to neither a summit nor a throne, instead as I float down further and further into the dense darkness the chords and the notes get progressively darker and more disturbing their tone is resonating and reverberating so much and sinking further and further into the beyond makes this more ominous, it would appear these stairs drift on further and further never having an ending, if anything this is where time, memory and eternity become one. Could I truly be stuck here for all this time am I lost to this world here on in? As the track comes to a close and those chords disappear into the great beyond, I wake at my slope once again. This was indeed a journey; however haunting a haunting, never-ending staircase seems like my idea of hell, so to be back at my hollow, torches burning bright and the smell of coffee and menthol cigarettes in the air a slight smile appears.","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-10-18T19:35:35+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepcofnEUki/","text":"Track five “The Last Watch” as the last and final track fills my skull with its beautiful tones; I feel my presence fading away again and back to the mysterious land that I’ve inhabited. Here this track is truly stunning and back to its lighter counterparts. There are some dark and deep chords mixed in here but nothing as deep as what was previous. Standing on the staircase I choose to go no further; I could travel this for a number of years and never truly get to where I want to be. So with that, I ascend back into the world above me and sure enough as I leave the darkness below me I re-emerge back into a hazy misty daylight. However, the bizarre part is I’m not where I was previously; the landscape has changed, the keep on the cliffside has vanished and I’m back at the shoreline with the misty pathway lying in front of me. I’ve witnessed the passing of time, forgotten kingdoms and ancient ruins all of which have been lost beyond this thick cloud of mist. So, rolling my last cigarette and lighting up, I float down the path that I originally entered on. My skull is now full of both wonderful tomes from this act and not only that memories of a side quest nobody expected, not even myself. Still, this music that has kept me company throughout has indeed fit all the segments of my journey. This piece is light and lofty; however, not to the point it’s sickeningly joyful if anything the synth parts kind of remind me of the track “Who Wants to Live Forever” by the mighty Queen. If anything it has elements of peace and serenity. It could also be explained as final music. After the journey through all that has happened we’re now at the junction we are.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-10-18T19:36:52+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Depcx-VEbef/","text":"We’ve got to experience something most wouldn’t have and that has been truly amazing. Reaching the end of the path the thick mist lifts and I’m back in my hollow; however, I’m not at my slope like the many times I’ve returned previously; I’m at the entrance of my hollow and I cannot explain why. Floating down the long tunnel I pull off my headphones and stare at my parchment the last track has indeed been penned, but how? In another time? In another place? Did I leave? What has actually happened? Sitting I read the last entry and it does indeed sound like me. Now I have had this happen before when I worked with the bards; their music took me on so great adventures and side quests. Something I thought was a one-off turns out not. Turns out this incredible piece by Spectral Vision has had the ability to do the same and for that I’ve been truly grateful.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-10-18T19:38:35+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Depc-e6kTg3/","text":"As my cassette player comes to its final stop, I slide my dilapidated headphones off and sit back at my slope. Staring down at the scribblings on my parchment I read back the various things I’ve witnessed yet have no recollection of. Bizarre but true, did I dream it, or did I live it who will ever know. All I will say is that this has been another wonderful addition to both my review portfolio and my dungeon synth collection. Don’t get me wrong I’m a mere peasant when it comes to this genre, not like some of you studious scholars out there. However, it’s a genre I don’t want to remain ignorant of, too. If anything I’ve embraced this genre and really delved deep and hard, going even further back than the first Mortiis album, and exploring more genres featuring synth, such as pop music, ambient, film scores and so on, as this I feel has given me a much better lay of the land when it comes to this genre. This is what I feel has given me the zest to write for such acts as Spectral Vision and the like. They are artists in more than one sense of the word; not only has a world been created in musical form, but also story and dedication to the genre. This creates a whole entire universe and with an older issue getting a physical release, this can only mean there is more on the horizon.","img":[]},{"n":14,"date":"2026-10-18T19:40:27+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DepdMNXkRe6/","text":"Wait I hear you cry; you’ve missed the sixth track. Well, the cassette doesn’t contain the sixth track and whilst it is an extended version of the fifth and last track I have heard the remaining three plus minutes tacked on and I have to say I prefer the shortened version. After listening to the extended version more than once I felt I got the end soundtrack piece from the first Terminator soundtrack titled “End Credits: Final Suite - The Terminator” by Brad Fiedel. This is just the softer opening segment rather than the parts that come after. I feel if you listen too hard to the extended version you’ll probably start to agree or see just where I’m coming from. So to avoid the album going off course the better version was chosen for the cassette issue in my opinion. I’d like to say that there are still cassette versions left of this incredible piece of dungeon synth, and there is. There is a solitary copy left. So if you want to scoop it be quick as there were a mere 10 copies created and if you want a modern-day take on dungeon synth done right in both physical and sound format then get to it. Until we roll the dice again and the bell tolls eight p.m my faithful readers, your humble servant in darkness. #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-02","artist":"Helcaraxë","title":"Anar ar Isil","genre":"Tolkien Dungeon Synth / Atmospheric","date":"2026-10-11","cover":"img/album-02/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-10-11T18:56:51+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXWoxxEeMh/","text":"1. The First Kinslaying. 05:36\n2. Exodus 05:34\n3. An Oath, A Curse. 06:03\n4. The Grinding Ice. 03:48\n5. A Sacrifice of Triumph.07:39\n6. The First Dawn. 03:46\nTotal Running Time:\n\nIt would appear that fall here in dear old Blighty is in full swing and with it has come illness. Yes, that’s right your entity is full of the delights of the common cold. So on one particular chilly, misty morning I sit sipping not only my daily dose of the black stuff, but also another cup of the good stuff, apple cider vinegar and honey, and not to mention a cigarette. Between that and a winter spice lozenge wedged where one of my teeth were in my skull. I’m feeling a little rough to say the least; I also hear the human folk below walking their animals coughing and spluttering like old-fashioned cars. So, at least I’m not alone in my misery of feeling like utter shite. I head back inside because I need to don another robe; the air is on the chilly side this morning. Donning yet another hefty drape of thick black fabric, my new fandangled phone sends me an alert that a parcel is set to drop. Floating back to my hollow’s entrance my phone dings again telling me my parcel has been delivered? Confused by this as when I get to the entrance of my hollow, the postie is there waiting with next week’s review material in his chilly palms. I stare deep into his soul trying to pry what information I can from him as to why my phone says I’ve had a delivery and how I’m receiving another. Sadly the postie’s soul is as vacuous and empty as his thoughts. Snapping the parcel from his cold grasp and a look of annoyance on my skull I float back to my slope.","img":["img/album-02/p01-1.webp","img/album-02/p01-2.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-10-11T18:59:18+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXW6qxkalM/","text":"Doomscrolling on my phone I see a post from the entity whom I’m reviewing, and this post pertains to his U.S fan base getting their copies shipped out. Seeing this image I sprang into action and messaged said entity about the whereabouts of my copy as it said I’d had it delivered. Luckily my dear dungeon brother responded with haste and had tracking on the item; he even confirmed it had been delivered. Scratching my skull I rummage through the pile of correspondence I’d received this week and I couldn’t seem to place it. Then the mystery opened up further when my dungeon brother sent me a picture of a mint called door? Now, it would appear that, that is definitely not the entrance to my hollow. However, due to floating around this part of dear old Blighty for the past five-plus years I know most places by doors and house facades. So, upon seeing the image I knew just what had to happen, I had to mingle with the common folk, annoyed at this fact, not with my dungeon brother but more with the total incompetence of the postal service and their total ineptitude at being able to read a label and postcode correctly. At the present time I cannot take a float down as it’s half past sparrow fart and I don’t want to be disturbing an individual at this time, so I decide to have another coffee and more cigarettes. The time passes slowly and I’m now sick of watching pointless reels of pets and humans doing the most stupid things all in the aid of likes. I bundle up and float down to this poor human’s house.","img":["img/album-02/p02-2.webp"]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-10-11T19:01:13+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXXIu6EVRg/","text":"I ring the doorbell and wait patiently; eventually I hear the lock on the door turning and the door slowly opens. The poor gentleman looks a little worried as, who expects a skull draped in heavy thick black robes to be knocking at their door at this hour of the morning, I show him the pictures upon my phone and he takes a great deep breath, thinking I’d come to take more than just a package; he thought I’d come for his soul, I did explain that’s the other guy in black robes and he carries a massive piece of farming equipment. He gingerly hands over the parcel and apologises for the inconvenience to which I write on my phone stating it’s not his fault more the fault of the shitty postal service. All people now relieved, I, with the parcel and the poor gentleman knowing his soul is safe inside his skin tube, proceed to float back to my hollow’s entrance and examine my package more closely. Yes, this week I am finally dropping my deep dive review on none other than Helcaraxë and his first full-length “Anar at Isil.” Obviously, this isn’t to get confused with other acts that go under this moniker as there have been approximately seven other acts of the same name. No, this is indeed my dungeon brother Gravfrost under a new moniker and recording something completely new and tantalising in the realms of dungeon synth. \n\tWasting no time I slide the cassette into my Walkman and slide the delectable rotting orange sponge headphones over my pitted skull and get ready to sink deep into this new adventure. First a fresh pot of the black stuff; more lozenges and cigarettes need to be prepped and placed at my slope. A fresh piece of parchment is rolled out and I can start to immerse myself in this tidy release.","img":[]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-10-11T19:04:49+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXXjIFkTFK/","text":"Track one “The First Kinslaying.” Opens with a deep yet muted bell chime followed by the sound of bats flying into the night sky. This then develops into the sound of the synth opening up for the remainder of the track. This is indeed quite the melancholic track and leans heavily into an almost gothic tone. When the synth hits a sense of grandeur develops and you genuinely get an intense almost Vampyric essence come with it, nothing too over the top thankfully, but you definitely get a real sense of it. There are definite traces of Gravfrost in this opening; the overall heaviness is definitely there, but what stops this from truly replicating previous works is the lack of distortion and other effects that he’s used. This is great as this is something truly new and different, though I do like the little essence of his previous sound peppered through this. As we progress lighter notes mimic an angelic choir sound that lifts the track in places where the truly deep and darker notes seem to be more prominent. The track is dark and forlorn; however, just take a look at the title and that is enough to truly give you insight into what this overture is to sound like. This track is deep and Immersive in every sense of the word and you truly get a great sense of woe and emotion from it. I love the deep and resonating chords that lie at the basis of the track and when combined with the remaining notes just a few fractions over you really start to get that medieval feel to it. You can imagine walking in either a deep dark forest or even in a castle’s deep corridors. Thick stone walls or trees covered in moss and a heavy moisture in the air. This is truly the work of pure atmosphere and it’s undeniably deep-rooted in lore and is part of the concept of this album which will all be revealed later. The track finally plays out on those deep dwelling bell tones that drift out into the cold night air.","img":[]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-10-11T19:07:15+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXX08CkRSd/","text":"Track two “Exodus” opens with the sound of heavy rainfall, sliding my headphones off a pier down the long tunnel of my abode; however, this appears to be a sample on the cassette. Synth is lightly introduced along with a sporadic yet intense deep beat. This amplifies the sound of the synth and the track becomes yet another dark and melancholic piece once more. Again much like the first track there is that stunning medieval quality to this and that only gets amped up further as we enter into the next segment. Here the synth emulates the sound of a hurdy-gurdy and other instruments of the time. This allows you to truly sink deeper and deeper into not just the atmosphere but the setting of this stunning piece; every instrument that is replicated here allows you to fall in love with the creation of this piece and what it represents within its story form. You can imagine the adventure that’s happening here; our intrepid adventurers are on their way from a dark place to adventures new and hopefully filled with light. Once again conjuring images of tall and grand landscapes filled with both treacherous terrain and many a peril. What truly does this justice is the power of the music; behind all this wonderful synth replication of older instruments is this stunning ethereal sound, both male and female choir vocals can be heard, the stunning bass and tenor of the male vocals against the stunning angelic female vocals. This I feel gives a stunning representation of light versus dark within the story and even the depiction of night into day.","img":["img/album-02/p05-1.webp","img/album-02/p05-2.webp"]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-10-11T19:08:37+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXX-4dkQrI/","text":"In the mid part, there is a real royal if not regal feel to the track and I love this; this is where the really medieval tone comes to life, the sound of an old rickety organ rings out into the air permeating such a regal feeling. The rain sample comes back and we gradually fall back to those darker tones of the track as it leads us back to the opening tone of the track once more. This has been incredibly well worked, written and produced. Everything has its own wonderful place yet it gels together to bring what I can only describe as a soundscape vibe. Once again the music does indeed do some heavy lifting but the atmosphere it creates for you to feel involved with that journey is truly brilliant.","img":[]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-10-11T19:11:13+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXYR7IEamg/","text":"Track three “An Oath, A Curse opens with the deep tolling of a bell and this drifts off into the cold still damp air; accompanying this are those deep male choir vocals that are almost at a chant kind of level here; this almost emphasises the sound of a soft yet strong wind in the background. Everything that’s in this opening sounds hollow and like being heard from depths below our world. It’s almost like the sound of an underground society crawling its way up to the light of the earth. You can tell here that this is indeed a concept album; the tracks flow together brilliantly and this certainly follows on beautifully from the previous track. So much has been taken from that first track and applied here and it’s worked. This continuation despite there being a gap between the tracks, is brilliant; had it not been you’d still be thinking you’re listening to the same track, for me this isn’t a bad thing and neither do I feel it should be for any other listener, here is someone within the field of dungeon synth who can write gripping and detailed tracks soaked in so much atmosphere you feel yourself going back and forth over that you feel you’re part of the myth or story that’s unfolding between each note and chord. Whilst these few tracks have had a predominantly dark overture to them I do love the glimmers of light that are refracted through them. From the lighter notes of the synth to the female choir vocals, these glimmers of light provide hope for our adventures. Once again in the really darker moments, you can really hear that Gravfrost influence.","img":[]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-10-11T19:12:49+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXYdtjkTha/","text":"This isn’t a criticism by any standards because I think what is achieved here is something different yet still with that gritty edge. #oo also of the opinion that some other influence has crept in here and rightly so, the likes of Old Tower especially, little hints weaved in and out from early works such as “Rememberance of the Lone Enclave” right the way up to more darker and oppressive pieces such as “Plague Harvest” and “Portrait of a Medieval Pressence.” Whilst these few tracks are wildly different they still share so much atmosphere and presence you cannot help but fall in love with them as much as you do these pieces right here and with that, the track fades out on the deep beat and howling bitter winds.","img":[]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-10-11T19:15:06+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXYuYuEX3C/","text":"Track four “The Grinding Ice” follows almost directly on from the previous track, you can hear those, bitter biting winds, followed by the movement of thick glaciers underfoot, this complete with the rumbling thunder in the darkness skies above gives this track a truly ominous and dark presence. Again the synth can be heard giving those deep chants under all the soundscape effects and this just doubles down on the true atmosphere being created here. Even when the lighter tones of the synth enter it doesn’t manage to lift the element of danger that our intrepid adventurers are in. This so far has gripped me in every sense of the word and here at this junction I feel like I’m slipping deeper into the lore that surrounds this piece.","img":[]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-10-11T19:16:37+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXY5fUke-D/","text":"Honestly, listening to this whilst walking in a forest almost knee-deep in snow would be a truly amazing experience. As I’ve stated this has been performed by my dungeon brother Gravfrost and whilst I’ve said there are elements of his original sound peppered throughout this release, this track here is possibly the one track that sounds like his previous work and not that, that’s a bad thing. Here you can hear elements taken from his “As the Mist Descends” full-length, which I don’t need to tell you is a truly amazing release in its own right. All that is missing from this track here is the distortion and gain in the synth. Here it’s much cleaner and more polished, but I do love the contrast between the two variations. His essence is still trapped within this new recording and I love the way it lingers within the darkness of the chords and the sound effects. This piece is like the pinnacle of the journey; from the previous track, their journey has taken them to a place that’s built of pure glaciers complete with bitter winds and treacherous thunderstorms. Every sound, chord or tone paints such a visceral image; it’s hard not to visualise just what is going on. All throughout though this track still doesn’t lose its medieval dungeon synth tone. I do love the effect that is added to the synth from the midpoint; a wavering almost wah-pedal effect is used to make the higher notes waver in tone making the element of danger just that little more ominous and the track also. Once again this is a fabulously dark and foreboding track; sadly it is shorter than the track’s previous but that just means I’ll have to play it over and over again.","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-10-11T19:18:06+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXZEavEfKh/","text":"rack five “A Sacrifice of Triumph” does indeed follow on from the previous track; those bitter winds can still be heard, but the sounds of horns can be heard in the distance breaking through. These horns start from much further afield but as our adventures finally make it from the valley of glaciers and more onto solid ground the sound of them becomes much clearer. The synth does an incredible job of replicating these horns throughout the track and adding lighter ones to give a more triumphant sound to the track, rather than the deep and oppressive tones we have heard previously. Again we have other medieval instruments peppered throughout this track so this still manages to hold its place firmly within the remaining tracks. I think with this track the sound truly matches its title. Whilst there is the sound of triumph throughout this, the almost celebrity sound of horns and trumpets etc, there still is a true sense of woe and loss.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-10-11T19:19:43+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXZQNKEdMY/","text":"Was a sacrifice made whilst out on the treacherous glaciers? Did we lose someone to the cold bitter storm that swept through this harsh and barren land? Whilst you’ll have to dig deeper into the lore that surrounds this release I can certainly say that it feels like it with some of the undertones of this track. There is a strong use of melancholic tones under all the victorious horns and that is more exemplified in the closing part of the track. It’s almost like a story being told by one of our adventurers about how gallantly they walked through the harshest terrains only to sadly lose one of their party to either the weather, conditions or even a curse. Either way, the track does a great job of both celebrating and commiserating the narrative. The track has its beautifully bold and grand moments in the opening first portion that sadly whilst being praised for their efforts get dashed as the story unfolds at the loss of one of their party; that is where the trumpets sadly fade and the choir-like vocals take their place. More sombre chords are laced within these vocals and it does bring the track back down to a much more melancholic and sombre tone. Soon though the horns sound; whilst they had a more victorious tone in the opening here they play to mourn the loss of one of the party and even now they fade slowly into the distance and we are left with the dark tones of the synth and the choir vocals. This truly does hit you very much in the feels, you genuinely get the feeling of loss and sadness, once again Gravfrost proving a true artist within the realms of Dungeon Synth. The track closes with the sound of our possible now lone wanderer riding out alone into more cold and uninviting weather.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-10-11T19:21:30+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXZdN4kcA7/","text":"Track six “The First Dawn”. Starts just where our last track left off, the use of choir vocals gives a true sense of space and openness that faces our lone traveller. However, in this track you now get the sense of a brighter future ahead. Everything seems lighter; the vocals are harmonious and quite angelic in places almost ethereal. As our lone traveller steps out into a new dawn he remembers fondly those he helped and those he had to leave behind. New and exciting times are ahead and this couldn’t be better signified by these heavenly tones and the sound of birds chirping. It would seem that all the darkness that he faced was worth waking up to this new dawn. As stated previously Gravfrost has orchestrated these tracks wonderfully and truly painted a picture in musical form of just what this couldn’t sound like if it was scored to the particular piece in question, truly something worthy of closing the album with.","img":[]},{"n":14,"date":"2026-10-11T19:23:04+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXZot2kThR/","text":"Sliding my headphones off I sip at my cold coffee anything to tame this wretched tickle of a cough, and I light up my umpteenth cigarette. You’d think these would make it worse, however, I’m lucky in that sense that they don’t. I have to say that this is a stunning piece both musically and visually and how can you say visually I hear you say. It is here I shall explain, the transition between light and dark throughout this album has been both well documented both in sound and soundscape form the use of music and the samples of the landscape allows you to create a vision based around the lore of this concept piece. This is where I’ll hand it over to the entity Gravfrost to explain. \n“This album follows the exodus of the Noldor elves following the harrowing events of The First Kinslaying, the arduous crossing of The Helcaraxë, the subsequent decimation of Beleriand and the first risings of the moon and sun above Middle-earth.” Now, having read that can you both feel and appreciate every note, every sample and the effort that has gone into this because, holy shit I can. It’s not often I get to say like I feel like I’ve been on an adventure with our heroes, but damn did I feel like it on this occasion. This concept the effort and appreciation of Tolkien lore has been soaked up and absorbed into every last chord and note played.","img":[]},{"n":15,"date":"2026-10-11T19:24:21+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DeXZyLtkZyr/","text":"If this isn’t in your top ten of Dungeon Synth end-of-year list then I cannot help you there, but it damn well will be in mine. This has been truly a blessing and an honour to review and it has had me on one of the best journeys since being with the bards of Gilgareth. Everything about this has been wonderful and the fact that whilst this project does indeed differ from both Gravfrost and Isbre, my dungeon brother has still managed to slide those Influences within this comfortably without it muddying or taking over this project making it a hybrid. It’s safe to say that Helcaraxë is indeed its own thing and long may it reign. Whether this is a one-off or there is more to come you best believe I’m here for it and I’ll be standing firmly behind it. Every project so far by this entity has been first rate and I’m truly honoured as always to give my thoughts and musings on this. Sadly only 24 copies were pressed on cassette and I believe at the time I pen this they’re long sold out, but don’t let that stop you from heading over and purchasing this piece because I can assure you once you listen even you will get lost within its true charm. Until the bell tolls eight again, your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-03","artist":"Undumë","title":"Ring the Bells: From the Graveside","genre":"Atmospheric Black Metal","date":"2026-09-27","cover":"img/album-03/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-09-27T18:59:43+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzT1eXkYjM/","text":"1. Ring the Bells From the Graveside. 11:18\n2. The Sky is the Pit. 11:15\n\nOne night under grey, darkened skies, I sat at my slope, tidying my last review. Cold coffee remnants sat in my cup, and a cigarette lay burning in an old skull; the smell of tobacco and incense filled the air when, all of a sudden, I received a message delivered by my feathery fiend. In his beak, he gripped a scrap of parchment. Puzzled, I took the parchment and read: “Turn on your computer.” No signature or name existed at the foot of this just a red wax stamp and in the centre it displayed the image of a skull with a crow sitting upon it. Looking around I thought this was very strange. Was this stamp a reference to my feathery fiend? Spinning on my chair I turned to my computer and switched it on, after many clunks and crunches, the sound of the dial-up tone to the internet commenced. The sound rang out down the cold stone hallways of my hollow and caused my feathery fiend to flee the infernal racket. \nOnce loaded another deafening screech echoed out of the tiny speakers piercing the cool night air. (I really need to learn to turn these speakers off when I finish with this godforsaken thing.) Opening up my messages there it was a message from the one-man D/S project Undumë. We had recently been in talks after I stumbled across his work one day, scrolling through the messages, he tells me of a special project that’s soon to be dropping through a newly crafted label known as “From The Grave Records.” However, mum’s the word at this point, and I am not to utter a word about it. Next, the entity tells me that something special will be arriving to me in the mail shortly.","img":["img/album-03/p01-1.webp","img/album-03/p01-2.webp","img/album-03/p01-3.webp","img/album-03/p01-4.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-09-27T19:03:04+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzUOD_ERuA/","text":"Days pass and like usual I’m concealed in darkness at my hollow’s entrance waiting to infiltrate the postie. The outside air is crisp and cooler than the months previous thank the dark lord himself and just as I let out a billow of smoke from my cigarette I hear a faint and meek “hello” stepping forth from the darkness my rotting hand grasping the items I feel the postie trembling. I nod my skull and give a gentle bow of respect. Floating back to my slope items in hand I sit at my slope and unfurl this brown package, stamped with record label stamps and little coffins down one spine, the other adorned with circular stickers of the entity of Undumë. I slide the delicate item wrapped in the finest hand screen-printed card complete with its own little Obi Strip all of which has been crafted by Joe from Grave Sticher and Vino Sangre. I place all on my altar and proceed to take photos, sadly still unable to post photos due to the huge secrecy involved, however, I respect this level of secrecy and the hard work that’s gone into crafting this item. Excited that I have had this in my clutches for two weeks now, I’m ready more than ever to give my musings on this incredible new piece of music from the mighty Undumë. I cannot wait to dig deeper into this two-track odyssey so without further ado I slip my orange sponge headphones over my skull and press play on my Walkman.","img":[]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-09-27T19:05:23+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzUe9DEX-k/","text":"Track one “Ring the Bells From the Graveside” opens with a beautiful spoken-word segment that, I have been told is to last the entirety of the track. \nClop.\nClop.\nThump.\nThud.\nClop.\nThe friar woke with a steady unease. In his confusion, he scanned his bare room. Saturday. The first of the month. Cattle day. People from all the surrounding villages brought their finest cattle to the village of Hethel for trade. A busy day, the village was awash with conversations of deals and merriment. Strangers walked the paths and soon became friends. Anyone could arrive, anything could...the Friar shook his head with agitation. No, not that one, that visitor does not come here. Please don’t let it come here.\nAVIS EXITIO\nEnough, he thought, as he prepared his robes. Though he didn’t get involved in the deals and celebrations, the Friar always liked to welcome those to the village and invite them to the service the following morning. This invitation surely acted as a way to curb the drinking that night. A person of standing wouldn’t dare enter the church with the memories of the night before ringing as clear as the bells themselves. The friar got dressed and headed for the door to commence his duties for the day. The music that backs this segment is ethereal, with higher notes used, this gives a gentle and light entrance into the track, I love how it ascends from stark quiet and just the deep resonating voice of the narrator to those glorious high synth notes, they continue to climb and match the tone of the narration beautifully.","img":[]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-09-27T19:10:33+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzVE3ekd3k/","text":"THE UNWELCOME GUEST\nThe following morning the holy man notices from his window the clouds in the sky. A deep\npurple filled them with the promise of rain. The purple led to a grey and the heavens\nthemselves opened up. Rain came down, shuddering upon the ground. Odd for this time of year he pondered as the air was filled with the smell of dirt, the rich odour of the grounds being kicked up by the rain. The morning, which held such promise, gave way to a quiet, drab feeling.\nThe Villagers darted into the church with a sense of urgency. No one was ever used to the weather for this time of the year and no one wished to be soaked to the bone this early in the morning. Though the oak door closed behind them with a sense of security, the people inside could still hear the storm rage around them. A murmur and hubbub submerged the\nroom and the bells played dutifully from the belfry. The friar took to the pulpit to deliver his sermon. The grass bent and folded around the graves as the figure slunk around the consecrated grounds. Its sickly frame racked and shuddered, its eyes fixed on the windows, the doors, anywhere it could gain entry. FRIIIAAARRR. Its voice heaved out of its chest and found its place amongst the clash of clouds and thunderous rain. FRIIAAAAARRRRR. The fiend cursed the friar from outside the church, almost begging him to come and meet him. Its presence was not lost on the holy man who delivered his message. Shadows lurked from outside and his eyes saw the figure from his pulpit. Not here. Not again, the friar thought. He was safe inside the church. He was safe amongst his congregation. They of course didn’t\nnotice the ghoul. They were too deep in prayer, too deep in the inward gazing. He was safe inside the church. It couldn’t get in here. It couldn’t in the old place either.\nHe could only keep them inside for so long. Dedicated as they were, the Villagers had jobs to do, the Lord provides but only so much in their eyes. His goodbyes were racked with reluctance and a wish to carry on conversations but the friar would have to say goodbye to the last of his flock before being left alone. Not alone he thought. It is still here somewhere.","img":[]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-09-27T19:13:39+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzVbeQEf7k/","text":"The holy man slammed the old oak door and bolted it. A satisfying clunk echoed in the small church which he could take some comfort in. Just as the ghoul stalked around the church, the Friar stalked around inside, examining every lock and bolt. Safe. Safe from the creature.\nReaching this segment we start to see a shift, again this matches the tone of the reading, its become much more bleak in tone almost melancholic not just from our narrator but also the writing of this passage too. Here we hear the lighter notes start to fade and the deep chords creep in. This again matches both the writing and narration as the mention of dark clouds, rain and the thought of a creature that has emerged, threatening to take the friars’ congregation. Knowing what I know about this written piece I have been entrusted with a lot of wisdom and insight into these words penned for the narrator. There are many metaphors within this whilst it reads like a story I think what the story alludes to to is letting darkness of grief in while the writer of these passages tries to remain stoic and strong. This is captured beautifully by both the reading of this passage and just how it has been penned. \nA DREAM OF FIENDS\nThe back window! The friar’s mind spun and raced just as his feet thundered towards his room. Crashing in, he lunged at the window, frantically scrabbling at the iron bolt. Sweat enveloped him. Stupid, he cursed himself. Stupid. Cursing lay way to relief as the holy man sank onto his bed. Safe. The ghoul let out a fetid laugh. The rain shielded him from view as he scaled the belfry. Bats escaped his presence as he invaded the space. His skin twisted and burned, a foul presence in such a place could not escape punishment. A price to pay the creature cursed as it descended further down.","img":[]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-09-27T19:16:44+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzVyDkEdIu/","text":"SOMNIA VENATOR\nThe friar’s nightmares were a place of plagued terror. The ghoul scraped the inside of his mind. Its nails, battered and old, tore at him, taking pieces of his humanity with them. The friar ran and ran down endless corridors and rooms trying to escape the beast. FRIIIIAAARRR. Its voice no longer willing the holy man to join him but rather cursing him. Cursing him for daring to escape him. The ghoul could easily catch up with him. Though aged and rotting, the foul ghoul was forever, its energy unbound. It was as if it savoured the chase, like it longed to draw out the hunt in the friar’s dreams. The beast lay bathing in the shadows of the church, its mind’s eye living in the friar’s nightmares. The more the old man suffered, the sweeter the end, just as it had always been for its kind. The ghoul let out a hiss at times, its mottled flesh charring in the presence of faith. It would soon flee from this cursed place, but it would not flee the friar. It has let him escape once before and would not let it happen a second time.\nOnce again this section delves deeper into the darker territory, the chords are now twisted and contorted much the same as the story. Again the writing coincides with feelings of both grief and horror. How the darkest of thoughts can invade the mind, having your brain and thoughts scraped away by what feels like dark forces, instead, it’s the intrusive nature of thoughts that the human mind is incapable of not thinking. The music that backs this all is incredible, rather than those high ethereal notes of the opening here the chords both billow and echo they’re long and drawn out allowing you to feel right there within the story. Like you yourself are being hunted or chased down. This isn’t just deep and personal for the entity who’s crafted this but also for the listener. With each and every passage comes a new feeling, a new experience, and this continues onto the next excerpt, here we see the arrival of lighter tones once more, this is relatively short-lived as the darker tones start to set in once more. \nRING THE BELLS FOR A DEAD LAND","img":[]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-09-27T19:22:46+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzWeULEXU0/","text":"FRIAR! FRIAR! A fist pounded against his window. FRIAR! The voice sounded out more frantically. The holy man awoke with relief and shock. He had escaped the beast but what now? The villager’s face, ruddy and unpleasant pressed against the glass. Walter, the village drunk rubbed his hand against the window in an effort to clean it of his spit. Quick to escape what had just happened, the friar sprang from his bed and opened the window.\nWalter slurred and hiccupped his way through an apology about missing the service and asked for counsel. Dutifully, admittedly thankfully, the friar let him in. Elsewhere in the church the ghoul slunk out and into the oncoming darkness. The friar peered cautiously out of the church window. The graveyard was filled with the inky black of night. Though no soul could be seen, the now frail thin friar knew it was out there. His eyes, now sunken and dark, darted around for the beast. The last five terrible nights had taken their toll on the now frail holy man, and he now feared his mind was lost to the dark visitor.\nThough he feared the ghoul, he feared more that his Lord had abandoned him, feared he had been left truly alone to fend for himself. An equally frail-looking but frantic hand struck the window. No, it was no hand. A paw. A clawing, skittering instrument of hate slid and tapped the window, inches from the Friar’s face. ENOUGH. The friar had had enough. The Lord has not abandoned me. I will not yield to a pawn of darkness the holy man proclaimed. His mind became awash with renewed faith and strength, as if the Lord himself had shone his light upon him. He spun around and thundered down the hall to the oak doors. His safety. His shield. He grasped the handle and flung it open. He welcomed his guest with righteous fury as he too became one with the inky black. A howl met with a bellow as the holy man lunged at the foe.","img":[]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-09-27T19:26:33+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzW6EJEcTd/","text":"Walter staggered up to the friar’s window and spied into it. Nothing. His mind fogged by last night’s celebrations could not spy the holyman, or anything for that mattered. He stumbled back and tripped. He soon found why he could not find the friar. His drunken mind took a few moments to comprehend what he was seeing; the friar, or what looked like him. His eyes, pale and sunken. His flesh mottled and bruised. A small trickle of blood was dried and escaping from his ear. An act of violence had been enacted on him. A heavy act of violence. Though Walter struggled to understand the act of evil placed upon the friar, he had noticed a small kink of the holy man’s lips. As if he had a smile on his face. Walter’s voice pierced the village of Hethel.\nSOUND THE BELLS! SOUND THE BELLS FOR A DEAD LAND. The struggle between the light and dark is quite obvious both metaphorically and musically. In this track, you can truly get immersed in the narration and the music. It’s deep and quite unforgiving in places, but I feel this fits the theme. You can accept this as a story and the music will indeed fit this perfectly, however, once you have a deeper understanding like I myself do it all takes on so much more of a stunning story. The battle isn’t just in the story but more of a personal expression, the battle between feeling the comfort and solace in religion when mourning, the change that can come to an individual when a certain stage of grief hits and finally succumbing to the darkness of it all, realising the faith you’ve always put into religion guiding you through a difficult time isn’t all that it’s cracked up to be and just letting the darker side of things embrace you with its monstrous claws and leathery wings.","img":[]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-09-27T19:29:03+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzXMWEEamW/","text":"You’d think here that the friar bestowing light and wisdom unto his congregation, would be the one thing that saves him, whereas it’s completely the opposite, he lets the darkness in and it consumes him, in such a barbaric way it leaves him with a broken and bloody body but that is not all, a smile is upon his face. Despite the horrors the individual has faced he hasn’t just embraced the darkness but also his own mortality. As the last of the dark chords fade into the distance the sound of church bells tolls; this doesn’t just signify the end of the track but also the end of this journey so far. We’ve been let in to a plethora of knowledge, feelings and discovery all through this spoken word piece and the haunting melody that sits below it. I’ve loved every second of this track, there is so much here both subliminally and supraliminally within the words and music. I’d like to give a huge thank you to John Brown, this is a close entity to that of Undumë and this pairing has been a match made in heaven or hell, depending on preference.","img":["img/album-03/p09-4.webp"]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-09-27T19:32:25+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzXk8sEdyH/","text":"You’d think here that the friar bestowing light and wisdom unto his congregation, would be the one thing that saves him, whereas it’s completely the opposite, he lets the darkness in and it consumes him, in such a barbaric way it leaves him with a broken and bloody body but that is not all, a smile is upon his face. Despite the horrors the individual has faced he hasn’t just embraced the darkness but also his own mortality. As the last of the dark chords fade into the distance the sound of church bells tolls; this doesn’t just signify the end of the track but also the end of this journey so far. We’ve been let in to a plethora of knowledge, feelings and discovery all through this spoken word piece and the haunting melody that sits below it. I’ve loved every second of this track, there is so much here both subliminally and supraliminally within the words and music. I’d like to give a huge thank you to John Brown, this is a close entity to that of Undumë and this pairing has been a match made in heaven or hell, depending on preference.","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-09-27T19:35:23+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzX6suEYTh/","text":"Track two “The Sky is the Pit” is strictly an instrumental piece and here we see Undumë carry on from the previous track the graveyard bells toll out into what was described as the inky black sky. The air is still and all-consuming. This track continues more down the path of darkness, there are moments where you receive little static interruptions and this has you feeling like your trapped In a world that’s shifting between heaven and hell.. Or maybe that hell is just more closer than it appears. This soundtrack you could imagine being used in either the video game realm of Silent Hill or for all you fans of sci-fi the sound of what the Upside Down might sound like in Stranger Things. The track is purely discordant after the first opening segments and it lends itself to this dark and disjointed atmosphere. Layers upon layers have been fed through specialist equipment to bring you this truly hellish landscape in music form. Even down to the point when at the over halfway point you can hear this set of notes that reverberate to almost sound like the American National Anthem. Obviously, it’s not because our entity from Undumë hails from dear old Blighty just like I. However, this surreal moment whilst not what you think it is, is draped in so much pitch and distortion that it just generates that vibe. Before shifting back into more dissonant tones. I love the experimentation on this track; it’s worlds apart from track one and truly lives up to its name. As the final seconds of the track signal the sound of the bells once more it is clear to see the inky black sky is here and it’s here to stay. It’s ready to sweep across the land and it’s coming for every one of you.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-09-27T19:37:54+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzYNKZERIN/","text":"As my tape clicks to a stop, I already have six cigarettes all filling gaps where my teeth once we’re.. And they’re all lit. I inhale deep and embrace the sweet menthol taste. I even finish the cold coffee that’s been sitting on my slope since fuck knows when. Honestly, what I’ve witnessed here has been a sonic masterpiece. The first track is a personal piece on more than one level, it is a fan’s love letter to Victorian Ghost writers like M. R. James and Edgar Allan Poe hence the spoken-word piece, but it’s done so beautifully and with true passion for that era of writing. Not only that but the music that backs it fits so perfectly; it’s a match made in heaven or hell if you will. There is also a degree of mourning in the first track as I have stated previously, I will not go into personal details as it is not my place but you can sense it in the way the story is told, the wording and the use of metaphors are truly astounding. I feel anyone with an astute mind can pick this up. This makes this track deeply personal to both the writer and the listener, because what the words don’t suggest the music truly does. As for the second track “The Sky is the Pit”, this is a true sonic assault on every sense.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-09-27T19:40:21+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdzYfC1kZFI/","text":"The layers and the construction of the track are truly something else and differ massively from that of the first track. If you’re a fan of Noise bands such as Sutekh Hexen or T.O.M.B then this would surprise you as it’s quite within that vein, while still advocating the spirit of Dungeon Synth. The one thing I can take away from this release is the sense of honour and privilege I’ve experienced from the label From The Grave Records and not to mention the entity from Undumë both of these people have truly made something beautiful whilst keeping that dark and sinister edge to it. If you haven’t yet purchased a copy of this release then I have no words for you worthless lot. Here is a DIY label and an underground act that have gone above and beyond to bring you the best in both sound and design. This will not just sit in my collection of tapes. Oh no this little gem is going to have pride of place in my hollow. Maybe with a little shrine of its own. We can’t bring back the DIY days of the 90s but with the right level of passion, care and dedication we can keep the spirit alive. Your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-04","artist":"Malgrim / Alogos","title":"The Shrine Within","genre":"Raw Black Metal","date":"2026-09-20","cover":"img/album-04/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-09-20T19:01:43+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhSgCwkecf/","text":"1. The Shrine Within. (Intro) 02:04\n2. Alogos. 05:08\n3. Leviathan’s Grave. 06:28\n4. MA. 05:28\n5. Titan Pentacles. 06:42\n6. … R.I.T.A.D. 05:31\n7. Lucifer - Demo 2026 05:03\n\nPacing the cold stone walls of my hollow it would seem that the closer it gets to the end of the year, the more reviews are slowing down, whereas usually I have an abundance that I feel I could never see my way through. Acts are pushing hard usually to see if they’ll make someone if anyone’s end of year list. Just when I think I’m going to have a slow close to the end of the year my computer gives out a shivering ping. Floating over to see just what junk awaits me in my inbox, it seems that a message has indeed saved this Sundays deep dive review segment. Opening up the message it would appear I have a message from one of the members of CON. Starting with light conversation and asking if I’d care to review their latest opus “The Shrine Within.” I quickly find the tracks online, slide my rotting orange sponge headphones over my skull, and listen to a few minutes of three tracks. Soon my appetite is moistened and not just from my fresh cup of the black stuff I reply instantly and say that I would be honoured to. We chat a little and the conversation opens up to the offer of a CD for me undertaking this task. Honoured, I politely accepted and for now I sit and wait for the inept services of the postal service, this gives me some time to do research on this act and find out just where they hail from. Initially hailing from Sweden this isn’t entirely accurate as P.N. whom handles Bass, drums, and guitars, is living in Sweden while B.L. Who handles Bass duties is in France.","img":["img/album-04/p01-1.webp","img/album-04/p01-2.webp","img/album-04/p01-3.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-09-20T19:03:12+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhSq4nEYmw/","text":"So flipping a coin for where I go to undertake this review is indeed a tough one.\n\tDays pass and I wonder if this CD is ever going to arrive, it would appear that two previous packages have struggled to reach my hollow before this purely due to the illiteracy of new postal workers, however more on that in the up-and-coming reviews. Whilst lurking in the shadows of my hollow smoke billowing out into the fresh cool air from my morning cigarette, I hear a faint yellow from the mouth of my hollow. Floating down I billow out my robes making my presence larger than it is, towering & floating a fair few inches over the new postie they shakily hand me the package, tapping under my hollow skull socket and pointing at the number outside my hollow they soon get the message they’ve been dropping the ball. The postie now sprinting down the hillside at the rate of knots is pretty impressive. Shrinking myself in size I float back in and catch my feathery fiend shaking his head, glaring hard at him I shrug my shoulders and explain that it worked putting the fear of god into him. Finally, with the CD in my possession, I can undertake the review. I pack up my bag my portable CD player trusty orange sponge headphones, tobacco, parchment, ink and candles. I float over to the map and look at my choices of destinations, do I take Sweden or do I take France, the flip of a coin will decide my journey. As the coin rattles off my cold stone floor and rolls away I see it glisten in the torchlight. It would turn out it’s landed tails side up, this means I will be heading to France for a change. I have recently visited some parts of Sweden in recent reviews so to be undertaking a review in France will indeed make a refreshing change.","img":["img/album-04/p02-2.webp","img/album-04/p02-3.webp"]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-09-20T19:05:09+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhS5HEEbcJ/","text":"I look back at my feathery fiend and once again he’s choosing comfort over flight, so alone I go. Leaping out of my hollow I take flight into the sky heading towards France mainly Paris. There is a bucket list place I’d like to undertake this review and I think my readers may know just where. Landing in Paris it would appear it’s a cool and gloomy day, but where I’m heading is dark, dank and filled with atmosphere. That’s right I’m heading below the surface of the city, the catacombs. I see a party of folk heading on the tour, I sneak in behind them and slink off down one of the many narrow corridors littered with bones and skulls. Entering into one section where the tour cannot go I nestle down between all the bones and skulls. I place candles out and create an even deeper sense of atmosphere, then my parchment. I am ready, with fresh batteries in my CD Walkman and the orange sponge headphones placed upon my skull I’m ready to delve deep into “The Shrine Within.”","img":[]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-09-20T19:10:15+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhTegPkZEr/","text":"Track one “The Shrine Within” Opens with the sound of the guitars in reverse, this enters quietly at first but then builds into the main body of the intro. Whilst wildly different this little moment of backtracking an opening riff reminds me of when I first heard Metallica’s …And Justice For All album and the opening of “Blackened” Little did we know that this would be the last great iteration of the act, however this is not the case here, the blend of the down-tuned rhythm guitars against the clean lead parts is truly masterful, this coupled with a slow yet slightly primitive beat is a great build-up to the remainder of the album. This intro continues to grow and a great sense of grandeur is building, the layering of the instruments and the way they are progressively building within each note played is truly something. This is possibly one of the best intros to an album I’ve heard to an album in a long time, it’s simple yet it grips you in every sense of the word, this over two-minute intro really takes you on a journey, almost like you’re on a journey of self-discovery; you are the person searching for the shrine within yourself, or even the shrine within this dark and miserable world we all inhabit right now. We’re all searching for that one place that allows us to be at peace.","img":[]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-09-20T19:11:45+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhTpdtkZws/","text":"This is displayed perfectly by the variation between the two guitar tones, the clean being the light of the shrine that seems to get brighter in both sound and interpretation and the contrasting deep overdriven thick guttural choking rhythm guitars against the tone representing the darker more bleaker elements of the world outside or the dark thoughts we may hold within. There is even some strong tremolo picking within this track that provides that cold black metal feel, but it still doesn’t fall into that freezing territory, there is still that element of warmth to it and this just adds to the overall atmosphere being captured in this intro. Here we finally have an intro that is worthy of the title, it’s long enough to captivate you and pull you in whilst also not overrunning to the point of surely this is gonna have to end soon. What an opening to this record already and we’re a mere just over two minutes in. Whatever this act is selling I’m buying and in spades.","img":[]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-09-20T19:17:06+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhUQoCEWAC/","text":"Track two “Alogos” opens with that raging tremolo guitar and some pounding drums, before we delve headfirst into the main body of the track, again this is a mini intro of sorts, and from this you get a very big presence much like the intro prior. The rhythm guitar almost has a tone that would match the sound of someone doing heavy deep chanting, much like a Gregorian monk. Again this opening is very primal but still retains that black metal edge. As this intro ends we get a quick glimpse of the bass guitar’s warm and relatively funky note structure as we enter into the main body of the track, soon a little drum fill enters and so do the vocals. The track keeps a similar rhythm as the opening and this becomes the very focus and drive of the track. As the vocals leach through the track, it is clear to see that they’ve allowed the vocals to be put into the background more and allowed the instruments to be more up front and the biggest driving force of the album. This doesn’t mean you can barely hear the vocals, oh no if anything this makes them sound twice as threatening they’re gruff, gravelly and forceful and if they had been more in the foreground with the style that they’re in this would’ve made the album practically insufferable, not to mention harder to pick out the musical patterns and styles. Here the duo have chosen to opt for the route taken by most black metal artists of today and that’s to have the production set up in this manner and it cannot be sniffed at as this works so well. Reaching the halfway point we have a lead solo appear from nowhere and this brings a whole new atmosphere and vibe to the track it almost brings a slightly eastern sound to the track. Sadly, it does overpower this segment and we do start to lose both vocals and the raw vibe of the track. However, is this intentional? The answer is yes indeed, here we move into a really intricate segment of the track, this is where the lead really shines, almost to piercing levels. The vocals come back and they get even stronger in tone they’re way more gruff and forceful and this works against the piercing guitars.","img":[]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-09-20T19:18:52+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhUdqREb2d/","text":"Once again this is a moment where the track is deliberately building and I love it, the dramatic shift that you would’ve put down to as an error is not that, if anything it’s calculated and well presented and leads into a fabulous light segment and I could hear the riff that was being used in my skull, but where had I heard it before and once again it was a Metallica track one that is again taken from their classic era Master Of Puppets particularly the track “Orion” this brief segment is either a classic nod or happenstance, either way it works and this is all before we return right back to catharsis, darkness, despair and those chilling tremolo moments. Everything shifts in this dying segment, it returns from its moment in the light only to be right back where it began. This track is so contrasting in its light and dark tones and it truly works. Here balance is everything and I’m here for it. It’s enticing and seductive at the same time and what’s helped is that there is some great structure and production. You can still have that cold crisp black metal sound, but you don’t have to sacrifice your musical integrity. If anything bring that with you. Work with it and craft it into what you want.","img":[]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-09-20T19:20:50+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhUr9pEZA5/","text":"Track three “Leviathan’s Grave” opens with a funky bass sound before we soon plummet down into the darkness of Leviathan’s Grave. There is some great use of a wah pedal on the guitars and this coupled with the drumming gives a great sense of tumbling further and further down into the darkest recesses of this act. Soon we return to those cold tremolo sounds of before, however, you can really hear the bass under the prominence of the guitar and this is the only thing that offers any sense of warmth in this opening of the track. As we head into the main body of the track we have a much thicker sound, possibly more blackened death metal in sound which fits beautifully and allows the act to transfer into those colder black metal sounds. The vocals have been kept to the background/ mid ground which is still perfect, as sometimes this can alter with some acts, not here though. Here the band have kept everything tight and on course. With a mini break after the first two verses, we get a mini lead solo that is blended with some fine black metal too. A little pause and we return to that much thicker and heavier sound of the track.","img":[]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-09-20T19:22:44+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhU5-IERlE/","text":"In this segment hearing the vocals as they are and reading the track title they actually sound like they’re being uttered from a grave or even a huge mausoleum as they have a slightly echoing quality that helps them overpower the thick churning guitar sound. Another pause at just over the halfway mark and we have back that wonderful Wah pedal effect back this adds a really dirty and disgusting feel to the track, like you’re deep in the mire of the grave. After this mini return, we head into another amazing soaring guitar solo this one way more prominent than before. Also, it reminds me of the technical solos used in Shining’s V Halmstad, and with one member hailing from Sweden this could easily have been a nod to the act itself, and if not it’s just another great display of the musicianship of this duo. As the last of the track plays out I do get the sense that there is a lot of Swedish black metal influence here, the likes of Watain, Funeral Mist, Marduk, Dissection and Shining. There is also a great mix of both black metal, DSBM and yes I’m going to say jazz, and before you start to look like me with your mandible missing you can really hear it in the bass guitar’s tone and style of playing at the end of the track, little flutters of smooth notes emanate from under those cold guitar chords and truly offer up an amazing ending to this track.","img":[]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-09-20T19:26:11+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhVTMJkcFh/","text":"Track four “MA” really brings into focus that level of Swedish black metal influence and you can hear it in the opening guitar notes from the offset, here this track takes a very similar sound to that of Marduk’s “Perish In Flames” from their absolutely blistering and possibly one of my favourite Marduk albums “Plague Angel” Again, this could’ve been a conscious choice or sheer coincidence but damn are the similarities there. As we enter into the main body of the track it’s clear to see the Bass guitar is the star hidden under those chilling guitar tones, again we get little glimpses of that guitar wah pedal like that of the previous track, this ties them together beautifully. It could be said that even the vocals are similar to that of Mortuus, whilst not as poisonous and vile, they do have their moments where they come close. This is by no means a complaint as I love this seductively dark vile vocal style. Because whilst they’re dark and brutal you can still hear the annunciation of both vocalists from either act. This track is much slower than that of the prior two but if you are going off the track I’ve compared it to, they are both of a very similar timing and structure. I do like slow black metal tracks like this, they allow you to sink deep into the darkness and truly appreciate the moments or bursts of light you get from other tracks.","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-09-20T19:27:34+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhVdREkaql/","text":"This one they have opted to keep well within the darker realms, where usually you would’ve had blinding guitar solo, here we are treated to a more dingy, lowdown and disgusting solo featuring the Wahh peddle and I have to say I love it and it fits right at home with the track, everything on this track is designed and orchestrated to make it feel as dark and as bleak as possible and then there is the outro… oh my days the slightly clean sound of the guitar twinned with the bass oh my days, I don’t usually gush over an act or the sounds that they create but by the unholy claws of Satan is this jaw dropping, well it would be if I had one. This segment is short but wow, from out of the darkness to this and then to return right back to that dark and overbearing gritty slow sound of the bulk of the track holy fuck. Here is a track I never wanted to end, this is a track I could listen to time and time again and never tire. This is beautifully sandwiched between three brutal tracks and it works so damn well.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-09-20T19:28:54+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhVnHvEUvh/","text":"Track five “Titan Pentacle” sees us return to the more fast-paced side of the black metal inferno to CON and after the previous track this is a little hard to adjust to but that is not a criticism, because, under all that rage of the drums and the guitars it is the bass and its level of funk that’s winning me over, those little root notes that break through the wall of guitar sound almost take you on an entirely different journey. This track is big and grand in its sound, with many different time signatures involved, from the slower more subdued moments to the full-on rage-infested moments that eclipse you in darkness. The vocals once again are powerful and set back in their proper position allowing the musical element of this act to do the majority of the speaking. This doesn’t mean that the vocals or lyrics aren’t intrinsic to the track because they very much are, they’ve just been given the correct position allowing much more to be heard and picked up on in the track. As we reach the halfway point more of that Wah pedal effect makes an appearance and it’s left me wondering if it’s going to be on the majority of the tracks.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-09-20T19:30:45+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhV0qEETFP/","text":"Now this isn’t a commonly used effect within the genre, especially when it comes to the more raw-natured acts, or even the more well-established ones come to think of it, however, here I’ve heard it used on multiple occasions and I have to say, on Blackened Heart, I like the use of it especially here. \nThis time though rather than it being on the guitar I think it’s being used on the bass and boy does it sound filthy, I feel the only time I’ve seen/ heard it used to this level was by Cliff Burton from Metallica on “For Whom the Bell Tolls.” Also by my favourite Metallica Bass Player Jason Newstead where he would use one in his Bass solos particularly on the San Diego dates in 1992. So, to hear it in these darker moments of the track not only elevates the track but just gives those nostalgic nods for me. In this particular segment of the track it really delves deep into a new level of darkness and disgusting sounds, but I truly love it, slower, grimy and dirty. All these words whilst used in their correct term would describe it in a totally different light to someone but here, these terms are terms of endearment for us. We soon return back to the main body of the track which in some senses I feel is a shame as to have closed it on these thick undulating grim sounds of before would’ve just been top. However, we do return to the original part of the track and where I said different time signatures are used in this track it’s the closing section where the track shifts effortlessly into something truly monumental, here we have this stunning solo out, again very Metallica-inspired, maybe even mid Guns N Roses. It’s truly of that era and boy did I not expect it. Did I want that dirty thick and disgusting outro yes I did, did I instantly change my mind upon hearing the outro I heard, yes I did, because what an outro it was. It hits you in the nostalgia bone like a freight train and it’s truly inspirational, I’m starting to feel more acts need to pick up on this act and quick.","img":[]},{"n":14,"date":"2026-09-20T19:33:14+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhWG00kT2c/","text":"Track six “R.I.T.A.D.” Opens with a swift drum burst before we head into the main bulk of the track. This sounds very clean for the best part and may I add sounds like a completely different act. Now, there could very well be a reason for this and all will be explained at the end. Either way, this track is different both compositionally and in overall sound and production. The vocals are much more at the forefront along with the guitars, everything seems to have a bit more polish on it and I’m not sure if this particularly fits the aesthetic of the remainder of the album, it’s almost as if the act has placed a cover song at the end, either way there is a distinct shift in both sound and presence of this track. It’s easy to see that there is an element of both blues and rock flowing through this track, it has a more upbeat feel. The vocals are still gritty and throaty, though right at the front of the mix, I feel if they’d been left where they were in the mix this track may not have been the odd one out as much. You see when you change the smallest of factors it can change the whole feel and vibe of the act.","img":[]},{"n":15,"date":"2026-09-20T19:35:09+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhWU7AEfWy/","text":"Now, where the vocals were before in the mix gave that very black metal feel, here now in this track they sound more Punk or even Hardcore, they’re almost shouted or delivered by force, don’t get me wrong they’re still within the vein of black metal but now by a much smaller dose. Reaching the midsection we get a slow singular guitar solo, this is much more the speed and sound of the band I listened to previously, and as the drums come in and the track gears back up everything switches, and we go back to the sound that the band had on their previous tracks, the vocals are back to being where they were previously, and the track has reverted to how CON first sounded to me upon pressing play. \n\tSo, what changed, what switched? Sadly those answers I cannot give you, I do have an idea, but I could be wrong. Either way, now we’ve returned to how this act should sound I’m much happier despite the expression on my skull which is usually the same as it always is. So, not only have we seen a production reset on the sound, but also we see a return back to those guitar solos that sound just like they did in the opening track, yes those intricate solos that sound like they should’ve appeared on Shining’s “Halmstad” are back and they’re just as beautiful as before. They also seem to lead into an incredible bass solo also, this was not on my cards but once again there is nothing that this act hasn’t done so far on this album that hasn’t blown me away. With the ending in sight, the track manages to retain that true Swedish black metal sound as we play out on a particularly dark and oppressive sound, even the vocals seem darker and much more ominous either way what started as a truly strange and different opening, I’m glad they switched it back up and corrected it, or if anything reverted to their sound. There is nothing wrong with experimentation but this late in the game on the second-to-last track when you’ve become accustomed to the act’s sound, it just kind of threw me through a loop. Either way, we seem on track for the finale of the album.","img":[]},{"n":16,"date":"2026-09-20T19:37:36+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhWm3jkVUf/","text":"Track seven “Lucifer - Demo 2026 does not sound like a demo, from the outset, the production is clean and the musicianship is tight. I do wonder though as there haven’t been any vocals as of yet if this is to be purely an instrumental piece. This opening has indeed some very primitive and quite tribal feeling from the percussive side of things and the guitars match up to this perfectly before it descends into the more black metal feel of the track. There are no signs of vocals and this music does almost encroach on the more melodic side of black metal with some definite nods to classic metal acts. There is a lot to break down here musically, so much interweaves throughout it’s almost intersected with varying influences yet sewn together to make such an incredible track even if CON only deem it demo-worthy. At the nearly three-minute mark though we have the sign of vocals, again they’re still in the original form of the mix just in the mid-ground, background but this is where I truly feel they need to be, especially with the level of musicianship that the pair have.","img":[]},{"n":17,"date":"2026-09-20T19:39:52+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhW3bokU5k/","text":"The track plays out on more of the instrumental side of things but Hell’s teeth it’s mesmerising. Once again it’s those bass notes that weave themselves between the tremolo guitar parts, it almost creates a natural high in me to pick out what possibly would seem like intrinsic moments within the overall sound, but here we are this is me and this is why I love doing these deep dive reviews.  Moments in music like this move me in such a distinct way and if you’re a fan of music and I mean a true fan, I hope that is what you get. As the last guitar notes fade out the silence of the catacombs soon returns in a truly haunting manner.","img":[]},{"n":18,"date":"2026-09-20T19:41:29+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhXDRRERBu/","text":"Sliding off the headphones the silence of being alone, far under the streets of Paris sets in. My candles are down to minimal light and sounds can be heard over my shoulder. Darkness seems to be setting in on this particularly creepy place, but as I place track four “MA” of the album back on, I sit and watch the flames extinguish themselves as the black candles burn down to just a mere puddle in this dust-littered ground and I feel at peace with myself and my surroundings. Despite being surrounded by walls of death and decay it gives you a strange sense of mortality even though I myself am not of this world, still I’m comfortable being around such. Fumbling in the darkness I find my cigarettes and light up, inhaling the sweet menthol taste, I actually forget the last time I truly enjoyed an album as much as I have this and that’s not to say I don’t enjoy all the albums I hear, but this one in particular has truly hit and hit different.","img":[]},{"n":19,"date":"2026-09-20T19:43:44+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhXTxPkTYI/","text":"Now, whether it’s the little nods here and there to eras I’ve lived or whether it’s the particular nod to the Swedish Black Metal scene, either way if you have to hear this or pick up a copy, then I will say you need to rectify that NOW. Yes, right the Fuck Now.. So, in the second-to-last track, I was going to mention a particular fact about this album and after hearing it, it took me by quite the surprise, so this album does indeed consist of four tracks taken from the first Demo, 1 track from their E.P “In Signo Draconis” and two new tracks. These have all been remastered with additional guitars and bass from B.L and you can tell, those added guitar layers and additional bass moments are what have made this album truly amazing. \n\tThis album was meant as a closure from the old but to also welcome in the new, which does also go hand in hand with the title. The one thing I will say though, is that the sound captured on each track I do hope they keep that overall sound because that is truly something, having already laid down bass, guitars drums and vocals, then adding an extra layer of guitars and bass on top.. what are we talking about here? Possibly the most seductive musical sand which possible? I’d like to think so. I will say, I’m truly honoured that CON, took that chance and messaged me for my thoughts on this because this without a shadow of a doubt is taking the top spot on my e.o.y.l. That’s right it’s up there, no questions asked. This both musically, in production and more has truly been something to behold.","img":[]},{"n":20,"date":"2026-09-20T19:45:06+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdhXdyYkRVV/","text":"I cannot even begin to describe to you the level of darkness involved in this incredible album and the best of it is they didn’t even have to try hard at it. No, corpse paint, no spikes, bullet belts or inverted crosses, they let the music do the talking and damn did they. Despite my thoughts on the second-to-last track this has been an amazing undertaking and I look forward to working with both entities in the near future on any or all projects. Stubbing out my cigarette and carving my signature F into one of the many skulls here in the catacombs, I leave the way I entered, for me It’s clear to see the shrine within me, but have you looked through the darkness of yourself to discover yours? Until the bell tolls eight one and all, I am your humble servant in darkness. #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-05","artist":"Pátria Escarlate","title":"Salmos do Naufrágio (Vampyric Nation)","genre":"Vampyric Black Metal","date":"2026-09-13","cover":"img/album-05/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-09-13T19:01:23+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPQ5cykYcm/","text":"1. Vampyric Nation. 07:14\n2. Quest for Damnation. 03:49\n3. Unholy Scriptures. 02:44\n4. Nihilistic Vampyric Satanism. 04:00\n5. Sorte Malvada. 04:13\n6. Peganvs Lvsitania. 05:20\nTotal Running Time: 27:00 mins\n\nWe are now classified as being in the Ber months according to the wonderful Gen Z batch that roams the earth making up stuff I cannot comprehend. All I know is the mornings are darker the days are getting shorter and I’m still itching for those chilly fresh mornings, but oh no, we’re still being treated to stupidly warm days hitting 24 degrees. I do hope this hot weather gives way soon to bitter cold winds, frost and temperatures in the minus’. Fortunately, I’m gonna have to rely on bitter, cold raw black metal to give me that chill I need. This is quite apt, as whilst I sit at my hollow’s entrance sipping on a fresh cup of the black stuff and huffing on my numerous cigarettes of the morning I receive a message from an entity known as Lord Cal’ Hav. Regarding the possibility of a review of his relatively recent EP “Draconian Methods.” Sitting here he tells me that he will be sending out a vinyl copy of his work and that I should be expecting it soon. Of course I am honoured by such generosity, and I tell him, I shall lay in wait for the postie until such time, this is purely due to how incompetent the postal service has been here in dear old Blighty, from me receiving other abodes’ mail to stuff just getting either totally mislaid only never to materialise at all.","img":["img/album-05/p01-1.webp","img/album-05/p01-2.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-09-13T19:03:28+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPRIo2ET4W/","text":"Days pass and on one particularly wet morning, I hear a bedraggled “Hello” come from the entrance to my hollows entrance. Wetter than an otter’s pocket, the postie grips what could only be what I’ve been waiting for. With no signature required, he hands over the goods and I hurriedly float away to my slope. Here I slice open the card and present myself with a stunning slab of obsidian wax from the Lord himself. I am of course referring to Opus Draconis. Now this isn’t a new release by any means, originally released in 2024 it has had three iterations it has appeared on digital download, limited edition CD and of course Vinyl. Thankfully I received the vinyl treatment, It’s not often I say yes to a CD, however, that depends more on what format a band has primarily released their work on. Looking at the cover I notice a stunning gold signature at the top from the entity himself and it puts a wry smile on this cold pitted skull of mine. Now, I have a little more information on the entity and his project due to the length of time he has been about, so how this hasn’t made its way into my rotting, achy hands sooner is anyone’s guess, but then how do you approach an entity who’s just a skull in robes?","img":[]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-09-13T19:04:13+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPRONbkZmM/","text":"Placing the vinyl proudly on my altar, I wander over to the map and look for Portugal. Now, usually I’d be the first to leap and fly out to the destination of said entity. Still, due to the horrendous heat we’ve recently endured and mainland Portugal being on a yellow weather warning at present, this would be a literal case of outta the frying pan and into the fire. So, I’m choosing to stay in my cool and climatized hollow and enjoy a relaxing day of listening to both the rain hammer down and what I can only hope is some truly raw cold black metal. Fixing a fresh cup of the black stuff and some cigarettes I return to my slope and roll out a fresh piece of parchment. Ink topped up, candles lit, sipping coffee and lighting my cigarette I place the vinyl on the turntable. I take my faithful orange sponge headphones and slide them over my rough skull ready to sink deep into this dark and brooding piece.","img":[]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-09-13T19:07:14+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPRkRXkYCc/","text":"Track one “Vampyric Nation” wastes no time in getting started it’s straight in with thick guitar tones, no elaborate intro with the sound of clanking chains, thunderstorms or lightning effects oh no, no time is spared at all. Here the guitars are thick with their tone and another thing to add in this ever-building is the clear and distinctive tone of the bass guitar, it’s heavy and dominating much like the guitar, its presence in this opening is similar to early Metallica especially the Cliff era, his Bass was always so prominent in the early recordings and this has that similar feel. Another thing to add is the drums, the drumming here is primitive and quite tribal within its sound, no blast beats or double kick madness as of yet but were seconds in of this already lengthy seven minute plus track so let’s wait and see what else is to appear, I think this opening is very indicative of the tracks title as the tribal sound matches the title, like the Vampyric Nation are a tribe of their own, as this intro pauses we now enter into the main body of the track, the track progresses slowly and with a deep ominous rumbling from both guitars and bass. We do get our first sample here, and it comes in the form of a spoken word segment “In the absence of light the darkness prevails.” This is a quote straight from the Hellboy movies given by the late great Professor Broom played by John Hurt. We soon enter into the main body of the track and all hell has indeed broken loose. Blast beats pummel and the slow grinding tribal pattern has now turned into a wall of noise. Raging guitars and bass have dialled up the speed to match the drumming. What can be gleaned from this is that the instruments are set quite far back in the mix, not so much that they become incoherent but more like a sheer black curtain has fallen and eclipsed the sky into total darkness. The vocals are raw and venomous, they have this amazingly high-pitched quality that leads off into a slightly gargled and rasping tone, making the. Different from that of most black metal frontmen.","img":[]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-09-13T19:08:58+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPRw7lEWE4/","text":"We soon reach the midpoint and the track takes a breakdown, here we get an almost groove-like quality to the track. The vocals are still, wretched and the guitars and bass fight for supremacy in their sound. It has to be said that the bass is the overall winner, you can really hear its thunderous chords against the ripping guitar tone. Whilst the guitar tone isn’t as sharp and piercing as what you’d commonly hear in black metal, it’s nice to have that warmer tone used. I do feel you find this with the more South American and warmer-climate black metal artists. Once again it’s nice to see that a black metal artist has adopted his own style and not taken the typical Scandinavian approach, here we have something fresh, yet still just as unhinged. For a seven-minute-plus track, you’ll be hard-pressed for it to feel like it is. This track is ferocious from the get-go and leaves no stone unturned. With wretched vocals, roaring bass and guitars and some stealthy drum beats this track will tear you limb from limb. I won’t lie I’m incredibly impressed, even more so at the relentless sound produced.","img":[]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-09-13T19:17:03+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPSsHhEfIi/","text":"Track two “Quest for Damnation” follows a similar path to that of the previous track. Here though the guitars are progressively getting colder. And you can start to hear that Scandinavian tone start to creep in, not just from the sound but also the tone of the guitar. The vocals vary between the wretched tone of before and also the tone used by Aphex Twin on their track “Come to Daddy.” Here the vocals have a much more audible quality than those of the previous. I have to say that upon listening to this track it does have a lot of Aphex Twin inspiration sprinkled throughout. I don’t know if this was indeed a conscious decision, nonetheless, it’s nice to see, even hear this. Aphex Twin did indeed have some truly dark-sounding tracks and Come to Daddy was indeed one of them. Obviously there is way less drum and bass influence in this track but little elements definitely lead me to that track. Once again the guitars are furious and played at a supersonic speed. I don’t know if this guitar pattern has been laid down once then looped to produce this viscous and cold tone if not, then a nod of the hat goes to the guitar work upon this track. Again the drumming is just amazing, with plenty of little intricate fills between the surge of blast beats. This track has truly quite the punk element to it, both the timing of the track and indeed the pace. This is a short track and one that tears you limb from limb. It’s viscous in every sense of the word. This is not for the faint of heart at all and I can say that hand on blackened heart. If you’ve never experienced either Aphex Twin or Black Metal then this isn’t going to be for you. However, if you’ve never experienced black metal raw, powerful and like a punch in the face then you’ve found your entity right here.","img":[]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-09-13T19:24:53+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPTliHkUuk/","text":"Track three “Unholy Scriptures” is another balls-to-the-wall assault on your senses. Again everything is dialled up to 11 and shows no sign of stopping. This is like a runaway locomotive and it’s barreling down the tracks straight for you. Much like the previous track, it’s relentless in its progression. From the first opening chord, it’s all go. Once again little flurries of Aphex Twin leach into this track, however nothing like that of before. Like the previous track, this track is very punk-oriented. It’s another sharp burst of energy and extremely visceral, especially in the vocal department. The drumming is exceptional as it has been in the previous tracks and hats off to the entity for this as keeping up must’ve been a true feat. Since its opening this track has shown no sign of slowing down, that is until we reach just over the halfway mark where the track enters into a little sort of surfer theme sound. Here the bass is the main focus and you get to hear this when the notes burst through. There is a slight surf element coupled with a jolly little punk placement. This does indeed differ from that of the previous track. With only one vocal scream at the end here, this closing segment of the track practically turns into an instrumental closing. I do like this as it breaks up the track nicely and does give you a stimulant break. These tracks are exceptionally full-on, and I get why. This almost feels a little like early Darkthrone, Burzum and even early Mayhem inspiration.","img":[]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-09-13T19:26:18+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPTv8dEWAw/","text":"There is certainly a don’t give a fuck attitude and I like that, I don’t use the early 90’s acts as a reference for sound etc, maybe a little Burzum in the vocal range maybe, here though it’s much more than that slight punk music attitude to the overall sound, the want for the music to be full of dissonance and raw unbridled anger and that’s the spirit that needed to carry over here. That nineties attitude and lo-fi sound has worked perfectly here, you can tell this from the outset. I can also say there are elements of later Marduk peppered through here as well, especially from the Plague Angel era and Rom 5:12 where the tracks became supersonic in speed and accuracy. It’s nice to hear that brought through here. Again not pigeonholing yourself to one particular sound here a mix of different artists has come together to create something truly raw and abrasive.","img":[]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-09-13T19:28:15+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPT-JZETk0/","text":"Track four “Nihilistic Vampyric Satanism” has a much more structured sound to its opening the guitars sound like they’ve been looped like mentioned previous, however, the beat is on point and you really get the feeling of this track being another relentless one. Its speed is unmatched, and once again you get that Aphex Twin vocal style once again. That grainy evil distorted and disturbing tone. Sandwiched between all the rage and blistering guitar work are mini breaks where you get a little musical flair from both the drums and guitars. This is where the black metal tone really does break through. With minimal vocals in these parts, you really get to see the musicianship truly shine through. This track is another short sharp burst of energy and that punk element really does come through. I have to say that a slower-paced track could’ve been inserted somewhere as it would’ve given a clean break between the really evasive tracks. However, this is still a truly great black metal EP. It’s different enough in its sound that it doesn’t try to sound like what has come before it. If anything it draws on the attitude and the energy of old black metal and to me that is what has been missing from some releases as if recent.","img":[]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-09-13T19:29:44+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPUJArkS17/","text":"Track five “Sorte Malvada” has three variations of translation from “Wicked Luck”, “Evil Fate” or “Bad Luck” so pick your translation if you will is much like its predecessor’s in sound, relentless, evil and uncompromising from the get go. The opening has everything going on in the opening segments from vocals to every instrument and it truly does leave you feeling very disoriented. However, we do get mini breaks with no vocals and it’s quite easy to see that the vocals are as big a piece of the instrumentation as the other main instruments. But when we get these little breaks from the vocals it’s nice to be able to hear the musicianship that goes on behind these wretched vocals. Not only that it has to be said that this track has more of a blackened death metal tone to it and that comes in the midsection where you get the breakdown and that wonderful tonal shift. Whilst only brief you can still slightly hear it buried beneath the vocals in certain sections. It also makes another appearance near the closing of the track. Sadly this fades into oblivion towards the end, I think had there been two breakdowns of this plus the fade at the end this would’ve put the track at the top of my list on this EP. This is the last of the shorter tracks and I will say that whilst they have been short sharp and invasive they have indeed been tracks that have pushed this EP into trajectories unknown.","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-09-13T19:31:04+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPUSx6EQfj/","text":"Track six “Peganvs Lvsitania” unfortunately I found it hard to find a translation for. However, here we are treated to something a little different the opening almost sounds like it could be a national anthem of sorts or it could just be a new composition by the entity. Either way, it’s a noteworthy opening and one that isn’t indicative of the ones previous. However, this isn’t to last as after 40 seconds were thrown into that all-out cacophony. The vocals and every instrument strive for supremacy in the more violent parts, yet when there is a break everything seems to find its feet and its sound beautifully and balance and harmony are restored. Again there is more of your traditional black metal sound to this track and this is picked up with the tone of the guitars. This appears every so often in select tracks and I think this works really well. It gives each track a distinct sound and allows those tracks to single-handedly carry themselves. This is the second longest track on the EP and despite its completely contrasting intro it does eventually go on to be as brutal and forceful as its predecessors bringing the whole nihilistic vibe to a close to this six-track EP.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-09-13T19:32:20+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPUcIGkSkA/","text":"I gingerly slide the orange sponge headphones off my skull and quickly search for a cigarette. Lighting up I sit back and watch as the smoke plumes in the air from all the exit points upon me. Going back through the description of this EP I come across your usual tropes black metal, satanism, vampirism and lastly Nihilism. Now the last term I haven’t heard in an exceptionally long time. Now, I’ve either lost touch with this level of black metal or there hasn’t been an act pushing this level of influence in a long time. If I think back, this is going back to some of the last few bands that had such a harsh Nihilistic view with black metal would’ve been the likes of Carpathian Forest, Urgehal, Craft, Judas Iscariot, Abyssic Hate, Nattefrost. Now unless you’re buying older stuff by these acts finding anything new by them is definitely not on the cards, so to see an artist pushing this level of intensity along with a Vampyric edge certainly brings a new slant in black metal. This EP has been brutal from the outset, and it hasn’t disappointed, if you’re wanting to hear something unhinged, full-on, and supercharged then you have definitely picked up the right EP, literally from track one to six every track pulls no punches, yet they’re still all individual within their own right.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-09-13T19:33:24+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DdPUj6Fkbzn/","text":"One thing I have to give credit to and I don’t know if this is actually classed as an inspiration or influence is the Aphex Twin nod. Now conscious or unconscious this seemingly influential nod is one that deserves merit. Not many acts can say they’ve acknowledged this act in the past. I can name a mere handful at best. So to see this come to fruition this late on is indeed refreshing. Another thing to note is that where I’ve mentioned Carpathian Forest and Nattefrost, as now, as I stare hard at the 12” cover it does indeed bear a resemblance to both Nattefrost solo albums. So again this could be a complete coincidence or just a great nod to an act that has long since gone to ground taking with him that truly Nihilistic recipe for black metal, however with some of these acts quiet at present this has left a gap in the market for an act to fill that gap and boy has the act done this well and done it with a level of passion and dedication to the genre that is well deserved. If you haven’t already heard this EP or any other of Opus Draconis’ work then you are doing yourself a disservice. Here is an act that’s bridging that what once gap and relighting the black flame for the genre. To a great black metal brother and fellow Lord Cal’Hav of Opus Draconis, I am your humble servant in darkness. #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-06","artist":"Initium Caerimoniae","title":"Initium Caerimoniae","genre":"Ritual Black Metal","date":"2026-09-06","cover":"img/album-06/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-09-06T18:59:08+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9PExmETzp/","text":"1. Initium Caerimoniae. 02:37\n2. Ignis. 04:45\n3. Arcana Noctis. 04:52\n4. Ex Inferis. 04:13\n5. Carpe Noctem. 06:27\n6. Occulta Suprema Ars. 05:23\n7. Finis Caerimoniae. 02:10\nTotal Running Time: 30:00 mins\n\nAnother Sunday looms, and so does another review. This one I have been meaning to get to, but for one reason or another something has kept me from it. However, this week I have finally mustered the time to actually sit and dedicate a segment of my day to pen this. This is another rare moment where the act itself sought me and whilst this isn’t always a thing, it has started to gain a little traction in artists submitting their hard work to me. I know it can be a daunting task handing your latest body of work over to someone to write about, let alone a entity that is just a shape in the shadows with a skull, but how many people actually go that in-depth with a review? Some I see barely scratch the surface, some just tend to write the same thing. Here though I listen intently, with moments of me pressing the soft orange sponge to my skull dialling in and really examining the tones, the beat and the vocals, I want the music to engulf me, swallow me whole, this is someone’s craft and like me crafting my review of said artists I want to show the same level of passion that the artist has injected into their craft. So when this act Noctivagum approached me particularly the entity known as D, I was more than honoured to take up that chance. We have had many a conversation leading up to this point and finally the time has come.","img":["img/album-06/p01-1.webp","img/album-06/p01-2.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-09-06T19:00:57+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9PSJgkQ1Y/","text":"Walking to the mouth of my hollow I go digging for more information on this act. What I find out after a slight error on my behalf and this wretched thing called the internet being wrong on multiple occasions is that a big portion of the band hails from Italy, while one member D now resides in Germany. So, strike one for my stupidity, now besides this physical release it is believed there is only one other physical release, that being a four-way split with Melethor, Antakala and Nefastus Sanguis. They also have a digital version of a Sargeist cover called “Burning Voices of Adoration”. Other than that info is indeed scarce. So, finishing up my morning ritual of staring out of my hollow’s entrance while finishing my umpteenth cup of the black stuff and cigarettes, I float back to my slope. Here I convert the files sent to me and dub them to a makeshift tape. Everything prepared I float over to the map, I’m now in a little of a quandary, I usually want to visit the places where the act hails from, however I have already paid a visit to Germany quite recently and at present I am done with warm temperatures so Italy is off the cards to. It looks like I’m gonna be staying here in dear old Blighty and as I’m just about to perch myself on the stool, the loudest crack of thunder erupts above my hollow followed by flashes of lightning that glimmer down the stone walls next comes the sound of rain, a torrential downpour washes over the mouth of hollow creating a waterfall. So that’s definitely put pay to going anywhere, so, with candles lit as it’s now dark and dismal (just the way I like it) I get ready with a fresh piece of parchment, my trusty Walkman, fresh brew of black stuff and countless cigarettes it’s time to delve in to the latest E.P by Noctivagum titled “De Arcanis Et Ritibus Sulphuris.”","img":["img/album-06/p02-2.webp"]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-09-06T19:03:55+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9Pn20EbLU/","text":"Track one “Initium Caerimoniae” which translates to “the beginning of the ceremony” opens with the sound of keys in a heavy wood door followed by the tolling of the bell. What follows is a beautiful acoustic guitar work. This is very folk influenced and you can hear this by just the way in which the rhythm it is played in. This is just one guitar at present, however, when the second guitar joins this track elevates in its complexity, now whether this has been recorded then double tracked and played over to make it sound like two guitars at once is a total guess, but none the less it’s a incredible piece and it truly draws you in. This is some exceptional guitar skill and musicianship. It would appear that this piece was done by Emiliano “Torc” Bruno of both Korrigans, Hagalaz and Antica Damsha. This indeed makes a lot of sense when exploring this as the first act Korrigans was indeed a folk black metal act. So, with all this in mind the concept of opening with this very beautiful folk inspired piece is truly incredible, even down to what sounds like some light Cello work or even more traditional folk instruments that lie just beneath those beautiful harmonising guitars just adds that added depth. This opening track I could listen to for hours, it’s so vibrant and not something you’d come to expect from a black metal act, but here we are it’s 2026 nothing is off limits, we have countless acts that have come before and brought us similar sounding music, but this here it’s fresh, invigorating and not only that I think this is the start of something much bigger, I feel in my bones there is a concept afoot in this album. Can I seek it out? and am I right with such an early call on this album. We shall see. The track closes on the sound of a fire crackling which then in turn leads us to the following track.","img":["img/album-06/p03-2.webp"]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-09-06T19:07:33+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9QCggEWo8/","text":"Track two “Ignis” which does indeed translate to fire in English follows on from track one and a pattern is definitely starting to form, as the sounds of embers pop and crackle it fades into this stunning guitar piece that has a beautiful echoing quality to it, this is short but also displays a greater influence once again than that of your typical just black metal. As that fades we are led into the meat and potatoes of the track, this isn’t your common placed sounding black metal those typical icy tremolo tones blast beats etc your average Scandinavian sound that just about every man and his he’ll hound has replicated over the years, no this is different, still following that almost folk vibe of the previous track there is a swaying pattern in which the rhythm guitar is played, again not your average black metal sound, this is thicker more girthier in its sound. It’s almost as if the guitar is following the pattern of the drums and bass at first, with additional guitar added over the top. This isn’t the usual structure in which music is formed but here I like how it offers up an almost disjointed and cathartic kind of sound. The vocals are very much in the back of the mix which is very typical of that early 90’s style of black metal we all know and love. After a mini drum fill, we delve further into that typical anarchic black metal sound, again the tremolo sounds from the guitars aren’t cold that they’re piercing, which is refreshing but also I’ve found quite common to bands that form around other parts of Europe, the drumming does indeed take on the same aggressive level of playing and we get some furious blast beats and double kick action out of the drums.","img":[]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-09-06T19:09:13+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9QOuykUhu/","text":"It is also within this black metal break out we are treated to that wonderful guitar tone in the opening seconds of the track, here, we get just a small amount of seconds along with a spoken word segment before we are catapulted back into the ferocity once more. The build up that comes here is something truly to behold, but wait we’re not done, just as I say that we spiral into a awesome breakdown, short and sweet we then head into a medley of beats and guitar riffs, and however many changes this act interjects into their tracks from tempo, to style to influence, it doesn’t deviate or alter the overall performance of the piece if anything it just adds to it. As the ferocious segment comes to a close we exit on that stunning guitar tone that made brief appearances in the beginning and midsection. This again is accompanied by a spoken word segment much like the previous. The guitar tones echo and reverberate both in their sound and around my skull, giving this track an almost Eastern feeling in sound, and as softly as the track entered it does indeed fade. This has been a helluva second track and one I don’t think I was quite prepared for. Nonetheless this has been a blinding second track and now all I want is more.","img":[]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-09-06T19:11:17+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9QdxakYAb/","text":"Track three “Arcana Noctis” can have two English interpretations, first being “Secrets of the Night” or “Mysteries of the Night”and this opens perfectly for its name sake, the tolling of the bell is back and the sound of monks voices echo out into the still, dark air. The track fades to a stop. Soon the bell tolls once more and the guitar tone that we heard close us out previous returns to us once again, this is beautiful and indeed a great call back to the track previous but also helps sew this album together amazingly. I’m actually starting to think that the idea of this being a concept piece wasn’t a bad guess at all. Again it isn’t long until we’re thrown into the brutality and black metal. Here the track has maybe a touch more of a thrash element to it or maybe even Melodic black metal, either way, like the track previous there are many changes of tempo, it shifts and changes at a moments notice but as I stated previous it doesn’t make the track feel unpolished or unprofessional. The track progresses and we get a spoken word segment once again, this time not with those stunning echoing guitars this time everything is much more throaty and dark which fits better due to the evil spiral down we lead into. We soon enter into another burst of blast beats and gritty guitar playing before the tempo changes once more back into the more melodic black metal sound, little glimmers of blast beats and brutal segments interject this moment along with some truly thrash influenced moments. This track is truly a compilation of influences all sandwiched together beautifully. Whilst sounding so sporadic in its tempo changes and influences it really does indeed gel well and this I have known to disrupt and cause other bands to sound slightly off, but here Noctivagum accomplish something not quite mastered by others. I guess this could be seen as quite the punk ethic but black metal is riddled with punk elements, it’s just here this act has caught the mastery behind it so well.","img":[]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-09-06T19:13:23+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9QtNAEWT8/","text":"Track four “Ex Inferis” in English translates roughly to two meanings again “From Hell” and “From The Depths/ Underworld” opens way different to any track that has come before it, this has much more of a death metal vibe to it and yes from this opening you can indeed apply that stank face, the throaty down tuned guitars and heavy thick drumming sweep you up, it could be said that there is even a slight Doom element to the tuning, and while I’m already to throw down to this opening their definitely needs to be the vocal use of bleurgh.. sadly though this is not to be the case, but the drumming in this bit is truly sensational, loads of bell ride and thick double kicks. This track has groove and we’re only in the opening seconds. Soon the vocals kick in and we have the use of death metal growls and throaty black metal vocals. This does work so well against the thick chug of the rhythm guitar, however when the lead guitar enters with the more higher pitched notes we get more of the black metal vocal style. Again the mix of these two genres goes hand in hand and in the midsection we are treated to the more dry, husky death metal growls. It is quite easy to say that this track is shaping up to be quite the crowd pleaser, this has changed from a mix of death and black metal to now being straight up blackened death metal and between a plethora of influences this does indeed work so stupidly well, now most acts cannot incorporate this many level of influences and have a album gel so well but here damn do they, this track acts as a great midway blowout, rather than having 7 cold black metal tracks Noctivagum have truly gone all out. This track is deep, rich and all consuming in every sense of the word even down to the final moments where we can hear the black metal throaty screams of Wolf before the track closes out on more Gregorian monk chants that echo into the black night sky.","img":[]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-09-06T19:15:39+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9Q91RkShT/","text":"Track five “Carpe Noctem” again has a double barrelled translation “Seize the Night” and also “Take the Night” opens with the sound of a wolf howling, apt as the vocalist goes by the same name. Here we enter into a much more melodic black metal feel of a track, the guitars are heightened and the lead is truly showing skill in solos. This is all before we are thrown into a chaos frenzy of black metal. Whilst this is said there are still elements of melodicism to be heard underneath the dark black metal moments. This I feel helps gel these segments together beautifully, it’s these little flourishes that make this track another mix that can stand perfectly fine on its own, even when the track slows and gets a little prog in style, maybe even dare I say a little early Darkthrone influenced. Then from outta nowhere do we get a reprieve, another spoken-word segment and a hark back to those slow and stunning guitar chords first heard in track two. Yes, they’re back and this time better than ever, the addition of added guitar is just faint enough that it leads you to question whether you’re hearing a set of female vocals. This is not the case however, the trickery that is used makes you believe that this is what you hear. This slow and beautiful breakdown is amazing and like when it appeared in the past has indeed been a highlight on the album. This soon changes into some truly discordant slow guitar tones that are drenched in fuzz and overdrive, smattered beautifully on top is both a spoken word part and the evil spoken words by wolf making this track truly feel like a battle between good and evil. The voices fade and soon the guitar takes focus, here you can hear piercing tones but the way it’s played against the rhythm guitar truly makes it shine, it’s like the track is being illuminated. The lead does take this moment to shine and amazingly so.","img":[]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-09-06T19:17:01+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9RH2XEUNH/","text":"The track is slowly climbing with stunning little solos sandwiched in between. This is the longest track upon the E.P and you can see why, a lot has been poured into this track and it shows, the time signatures are a lot less erratic, everything has time to grow and blossom, and this by no means is a slant on the tracks previous because this just goes to show that the band can accomplish both. This moment whilst it’s been beautiful, breaks, and stunningly, more darker black metal moments once more and with haste and speed may I add. Once again there are dealing vocals and the difference still sounds like the battle between good and evil, whilst the spoken word part struggles to be heard it’s the dark demonic vocals that prevail and extinguish the vocals of the monk. The ending of the track is a great mix of both melodic black metal with some slithers of thrash just enough to grip the listener to the closing moments of the track.","img":[]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-09-06T19:19:35+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9RakEEWuQ/","text":"Track six “Occulta Suprema Ars.” Is another track that has a double barrelled translation “The Supreme Hidden Art” or “The Highest Secret Art” starting slow with those stunning echoing guitar tones that I have eventually found sound a little like The Cult’s (Don’t Fear) The Reaper. They truly sound like heaven ascending but with the lighter chimes behind them, this isn’t surprising. What I do love is the hot molten injection of furious black metal that follows. This is relentless and truly a case of darkness snuffing out the light, here everything is on point from the guitars working in total unison to the awesome drumming, this moment right here hits hard. Even when the tempo slightly shifts and the track takes a slow breakdown there is just something here that makes this track truly sublime. Whilst this lead up to the midsection sounds disjointed what it later shifts into is exquisite the sound of the acoustic guitars and those echoing electric chords behind just become sublime, with a little Latin spoken word part, this little light segment soon changes and we enter into the colder black metal moment complete with diabolical screams and howls. The track soon merges back to that powerful part of the intro, all hell breaks loose and the track enters into one of the coldest moments on the album. Here the tremolo picking is dialled up to eleven and it truly shows, that is before it falls back into this rolling thick barrage of sound, but we’re not done here like all the previous tracks Noctivagum have more tempo changes hidden up their black sleeves, things slow right back down and those piercing tremolo notes come rushing back in, it’s like a lead guitar solo almost just slowed down and more sorrowfully played, the notes truly linger and stab at you, that is until the calm of the ending swirls in and takes you away, again a Latin spoken word part enters the realm once more truly giving this track it’s rightful name “The Supreme Hidden Art.”","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-09-06T19:23:25+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9R2sHEdA-/","text":"Track seven “Finis Caerimoniae” or “End of the Ceremony” brings us right back to more beautiful folk acoustic work from Emiliano “Torc” Bruno once again, yes, that’s right the person who crafted the stunning opening intro also recorded this segment. However, try as I might this opening piece sounds like something I’ve heard before and as I shake the grey matter and dust that’s in my skull I cannot seem to guess it. I’m sure I’ll be sat silent late one evening and it’ll come back to me. Either way, you can really hear the additional folk instruments in this piece I’m sure whilst not common to most Italian folk music examples of a harpsichord or Hardanger or even a Tagelharpa can be heard. I may be wrong on this completely, but it does have that Scandinavian influence smeared in. This track is another stunning example of musicianship much the same as the intro. This outro though is a much more lively affair and could quite induce a jig or indeed some merry ment either way it’s a stunning way to finish this album. As the keys can be heard turning in the lock once more the last notes to fade off into the distance is the tolling of the bell once again. Following the same path as that of the intro, bringing this E.P full circle and to the best close possible.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-09-06T19:26:25+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9SMmMkVEv/","text":"Sitting here and sliding my headphones off my skull, I’m truly appreciative of this act getting in touch and bridging a gap. I’ve had so much fun listening to this project and the potential that’s truly locked in here. There’s a lot to digest in this E.P and I know that going in for half a listen won’t do you any favours. This is an E.P you need to lock in on. Take the time really focus on every little detail from the intro right the way through, there’s just so much even with every tempo change if your not paying attention this will appear jarring and unprovoked. Where, when you listen and truly zone in you can see that these turbulent tempo changes keep the listener engaged and drawn in this then allows for the concept behind the E.P to become visualised and I will leave it up to you the listener what the concept behind this E.P is. Another thing to note is when I mentioned full circle moments regarding the tracks there is a little more beneath this act than even I first thought. Obviously it is D and it’s his project and vision. The vocals, guitars and drums and bass have all been provided by a set of individuals and one in particular caught my cold steely gaze that being of course the vocalist known as Wolf. His vocals started sounding a little familiar and after more digging and messaging between D and I, it came to fruition that Wolf was indeed part of another Black Metal act known as “Gort” now if you’ve been around as long as I have within this industry this name should in fact mean something. So, along time ago when I was a mere spectre wondering this earth I did infact have great contact with the band Gort, I also reviewed there work twice in my early years so between penning this I shot some photos over to D showing my two very Early Gort cassettes.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-09-06T19:27:52+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Dc9SXPoEbn-/","text":"Of course this would appear to bear very little meaning to the E.P in question, however like the E.P came full circle in its sound so has a part of my existence on this earth. You see music has always been the one constant in my existence no matter what, no matter where I go I’ve always had it as a faithful companion, people, friends family, have all come and gone, hell I’ve even existed long since past people have expired, but it’s strange that on a random approach from an entity known as D would he bring not just an incredible EP full circle but also something in my intrinsic existence. So with that all said I bid you all farewell this Sunday evening and until the bell tolls eight once more. Your humble servant in darkness. #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-07","artist":"A Funeral of Being","title":"A Funeral of Being","genre":"DSBM (Depressive Suicidal Black Metal)","date":"2026-08-23","cover":"img/album-07/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-08-23T18:59:41+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZMAldEZAb/","text":"1. A Funeral of Being. 04:20\n2. Thoughts After The Gun Jams. 04:00\n3. Hurt. 04:40\n4. Broken Trust. 02:30\n5. Life Has Always Been This Shade. 04:01\n6. Ashes of my Former Self. 04:11\n7. No Point in Suffering. 04:47\n8. Forgotten. 04:05\n9. Emptiness. 02:56\nTotal Running Time. \n\nWhilst the heat wave was in full effect until recently and the postal service has been in mid-collapse, I had to send my feathery fiend out on an errand with strict instructions. As I stared deep into his glossy yet obsidian eyes, I signed out to him to stay hydrated, take regular breaks and to pick up a special red package from the Matriarch herself. Hopping into my robes, I performed my morning ritual: making a fresh batch of the black stuff and enough menthol cigarettes to keep my sanity in check for his impending venture. With one last nod at one another, the mission was understood. He took off into the blue skies to retrieve this special little package. Time fluttered by and eventually he returned, landing on my slope he dropped the red package into the rotting flesh of my hands. Placing it on my altar behind me for a quick photo session I carefully unwrap the delicate red paper to reveal the cassette for this review. It is none other than My Last Promise and her first full-length titled “A Funeral of Being.” First under the impression this young woman hailed from dear old Blighty much like Harifa and yours truly penning this, as at present there seems to be a plethora of single female DSBM acts surfacing from our tiny island. However, I was happy to be proven wrong and after a little research, I found that this young woman hails from Ukraine. Now there have been many great acts hailing from this part of the world such as Nokturnal Mortum, Drudkh, Hate Forest, Kroda and the mighty Altar in Darkness (R.I.P.) to name just a few. So, to see a single female-fronted DSBM act rise from this area of the world is not just a pleasant surprise, but also a welcome one.","img":["img/album-07/p01-1.webp","img/album-07/p01-2.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-08-23T19:02:30+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZMVNXEVhK/","text":"Having touched lightly on the fact that this one-woman act is a DSBM project you can imagine the hardship already that is to be felt upon this release. I so want to slide this cassette into my new shiny Walkman already but I feel there is not just something stirring beneath one’s black heart but outside too. I speedily float to my hollow’s entrance where the wind has risen considerably and the biggest darkest black clouds have begun to form. Is this the coming of the first downpour in nearly three months straight of no rain? The sky has turned from blue to almost pitch black, hints of red still tinge the skies, like the sun is being blotted out, when all of a sudden fork lightning rips through and lights up the dark ominous skies. Finally, the little droplets fade and huge almost golf ball-sized ones start to fall. Standing at the mouth of my hollow I revel in the smell of fresh soil being churned up by the deluge of rain, the heavy rumbles of thunder and the lightning bearing down on my hollow. Is this a sign of things to come? Has the arrival of this dark and intriguing cassette brought this change in weather? Either way, I waste no time in rushing to the Map that adorns my hollow’s wall. With no exact point of reference as to where the act hails from in Ukraine, I pick up my bag and stuff all that I need into it. \n\tWith everything packed I quickly float to my hollow’s entrance, looking back at my feathery fiend, he just shrugs his wings and I leap and take flight. Passing over the countries that neighbour Ukraine, I finally touch down in Kyiv. Once a stunning place to visit it now resembles a post-apocalyptic landscape, buildings and roads laid to waste, the skies filled with billowing smoke. This is nothing short of horrifying, how can one country do this to another?","img":[]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-08-23T19:03:27+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZMcKBEVHz/","text":"As the ground beneath begins to shake I make a quick decision to delve into a building that has seen better days, riddled with mortar holes this building represents Swiss cheese more than it does a building. As the sun begins to set and darkness now becomes my friend once again, I hunker down amongst the rubble and debris that is left. I light small candles and fashion a makeshift flat surface to lay my parchment out onto. With my Walkman at hand I think playing this at a lower volume may benefit me, that way I can still sense any impending danger should there be any. All set I slide the fresh orange sponge headphones over my skull and get ready to delve deep into this release hopefully this won’t be my Funeral of Being.","img":[]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-08-23T19:06:22+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZMxnGETYQ/","text":"Track one “A Funeral of Being” is a slight play on the album title and this opening track name, originally used by the mighty Xasthur for his album “The Funeral of Being” This does indeed indicate just what the listener may be ready to experience when pressing play on this release. As the opening guitar chords slowly bleed out into my skull soft vocals accompany it, this is peak DSBM at its finest, drenched in melancholy and sadness this intro truly feels like it’s leading into something truly spectacular. The track still remains on a truly haunting trajectory with the guitars now dipping into colder and frostier tones. The only warmth you feel at this point is the bass, here the bass provides warm and almost comforting tones, like someone wrapping a thick blanket around you. Then, without warning we delve deep into the darker and bleaker sound. The vocals shift from a soft angelic style to one of harsh bitterness and agony, they’re husky and strained and the real agony of Haze can be felt. The drumming is kept nice and simple throughout, a consistent double kick with stunning little fills in between keeps you engaged, whilst the guitars range from your standard tremolo black metal-drenched sound when the track elevates in places, but between those moments, woven in are the stunning melancholic riffs of the intro.","img":[]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-08-23T19:08:19+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZM_4WEbrQ/","text":"Another thing to add is that the overlapping vocal qualities on this track between the bitter brutal and harsh vocals, are a soft-spoken word segment that works really well. The track is really well thought out and well paced, the mix of light and dark, tones truly complements one another and also gives way to one another so well. This track being the first off the album and indeed the title track of the album truly shows a well-presented start to the album. The song ends on a gradual sombre ending and the sound of light rain and thunder can be heard. Finally, as the track comes to an end amidst the sound of pouring rain you hear a final scream from the dark queen of this release. This truly leaves a harrowing and lasting effect as the sound of the rain continues to pour down.","img":[]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-08-23T19:12:11+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZNcMkkUZe/","text":"Track two “Thoughts After the Gun Jams” wastes no time in getting straight to the point and if the title wasn’t enough of a hint then this opening certainly will be. The harrowing screams from Haze echo through my skull along with what sounds like piano keys, played in a very extreme manner that manage to bleed into the rhythm guitars perfectly, while the lead guitar almost acts like razor wire through flesh, cold calculating and visceral, the only let-up from this truly agonising track is the drum beat and bass. Here these two instruments work side by side keeping the tempo thick and slightly viscous in consistency. Unlike the track before where there was a mix between, the serene and the agony, this isn’t the case with this track, this is full on misery and sadness in absolutely everything, the beat acts like a driving force behind getting a gun and ending your own life, that coupled with chilling tremolo guitars just truly drapes your mood and the listener in thick black cloth in which no light can pass. This is a truly valiant and brutal track and the ability to nail the vocals the way Haze has is truly astonishing. This track is relentless in its sound if you thought other acts were advocates for listeners taking their own lives or showing true emotion through both sound and effects then you better be ready for this contender because there is something in this track that could easily lead you down this path.","img":[]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-08-23T19:13:42+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZNnRkEXe4/","text":"This track is not for the faint of heart and how it must’ve felt to record this, must’ve been a real hard slog, because the emotion that rides through this track is truly astonishing, not only that but the level of loneliness and isolation it offers is truly some of the best I’ve heard. The track ends on some distorted notes and into some much cleaner drumming and slower guitar work. There is a slight discordance to this ending it’s almost like it’s deliberately played in a off timing which almost nods towards a slight jazz influence. However, all in all, it fits the vibe of the overall track and is something that is often seen in some DSBM bands, especially the likes of Shining and Thy Light.","img":[]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-08-23T19:16:25+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZN7OCEZ4K/","text":"Track three “Hurt” is a world apart from its predecessor, and where I was about to say this isn’t a Johnny Cash cover or Nine Inch Nails for that matter, however with its almost semi-acoustic opening you could almost be led to think that this is indeed where the track is heading. Instead, this soft and very melancholic sound backed by Haze’s vocals and soft whimpers really does bring out the discomfort and newer levels of depression. This segment almost reminds me of the spoken-word segment taken from Shining’s “Låt OSS Ta Allt Från Varandra” taken from the album V. Halmstad. That moment where the spoken word part comes in at 1:53 really does deliver the element of sadness much like the opening of this track. Another thing to note is when the bass hits, those long, drawn-out, deep rumbling notes echo in a similar vein to that of Metallica’s “My Friend of Misery,” and if you actually slowed the aforementioned track down the similarities would be quite uncanny. This slow and yet somewhat quiet intro is definitely a ruse as soon as it ends we’re thrown into the harsher elements, this comes more in the form of the vocals rather than the instruments, what I can glean firsthand is that while the lead is a clean sound, the rhythm is the guitar that has the most overdrive and distortion on it. This is most prevalent over the halfway mark, here there is. A gradual build and pace change, the lead guitar climbs to higher and higher pitches in tremolo picking, even the drumming moves from a slow and steady pace to a more galloping one. This pace is only reserved for the midsection and as the track descends into its final notes it is the lead guitar that plays us out on some sombre chords along with some agonising screams of why by Haze closing the track in an incredible way.","img":[]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-08-23T19:19:04+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZOOnXERgH/","text":"Track four “Broken Trust” starts with a great piano moment that once again leads the grey dust particles in my skull to thinking about the piano parts on Shining’s V. Halmstad, particularly “Ttiosextusenfyrahundra”. This has become quite the inspiration for the DSBM bands since its release and it is quite clear to see that this is the same here. Whilst this doesn’t lead off into Beethoven’s “Moonlight Sonata” like that of the track by Shining, it is clear to see the influence of having a piano on a DSBM album. It is played slowly and with quite a hard hand, you can really hear how the notes vibrate and resonate in the air. There are even moments where some notes sound quite out of key with the melody, whether this was intentional, to add a level of dissonance to the track I don’t know, either way it actually adds to the overall atmosphere. This is the shortest track amidst tracks that are of average length and so far seems to be the only instrumental piece so far. Either way, it offers a good reprieve from the solemn and harsher tones of the album.","img":[]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-08-23T19:22:15+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZOl5QkaL1/","text":"Track five “Life Has Always Been This Shade” opens in a slightly similar manner to that of the opening track. It’s slow and offers that haunting melody at first with those clean vocals, this doesn’t indeed shift into the bitter and dark side of the track, not even gradually as soon as that first scream echoes out you know you’re ready to spiral into the darkest parts once more. I love once again the use of the clean angelic vocals next to the harsher and more brutal ones. Whilst I said in a previous review about more female DSBM singers needing to give a nod to Grushenka of Dysthymia, this I feel is one of those cases as you really get to hear the influence here on this track. Like that of the Dysthymia vocal styles, there is a mix of grunge elements here and it should definitely be duly noted. This is merely in the midsection of the track as when we reach over the halfway mark the track takes a completely different spin. The drumming becomes much darker and heavier, the guitars layer one on top of another to form this almost spiralling and falling-into-darkness atmosphere. This is by no means a bad thing, it’s something just to be noted with the trajectory of the track. Whilst before the piano parts of the prior track were somewhat uplifting this is completely the opposite and reflects more of the descent into isolation and loneliness.  Again this seems to be a short track but when clocked up to the others it’s actually quite the same. I think having the different segments and paces truly engages the listener and before you know it the tracks are over and done, not like some tracks that can just do you over.","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-08-23T19:24:04+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZOzN5kfYU/","text":"rack six “Ashes of my Former Self” starts with a beautiful guitar intro and those soft vocals once more, however, I know this isn’t to last, and sure enough, as we hit the first verse everything amps up and takes you once again on that truly dark journey. One thing I will add though is that behind all the loud and in your face elements is either the lead or rhythm guitar plays a structured chord that you can hear beneath the harsh vocals and it reminds me of two different tracks, and these both appear on movie soundtracks Lock Stock and Two Smoking Barrels that being “I Wanna Be Your Dog”  by The Stooges or even the theme from 28 Days Later by John Murphy. You can really hear light elements of those tracks here on this and I know the 28 Days theme music has been quite a well-documented one in my reviews, however here on this piece, coupled with the surroundings I’m in it feels somewhat appropriate. As we enter into the midsection the atmosphere ramps up and everything comes together, but the guitar solo that challenges the harsh vocals is truly something, especially against the rolling drumming and bass. Here once again you get glimmers of them pushing through and it truly helps the track develop some warmth especially with the subject matter being a cold and stripped one. This is one of the harshest tracks on the entire album and how this young female has trained her vocals to do what they can do is nothing short of astounding. Here is where you get the full rage of ferocity, this track is in your face and hides nothing. It’s well constructed and executed down to the very last note.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-08-23T19:26:41+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZPGWxEeLj/","text":"Track seven “No Point in Suffering” opens similarly to some of the softer-sounding tracks, however this one slightly differs in a way. Here we have a track that sounds slightly very indie-oriented. I would dare to go as far as saying it sounds like a very slow representation of Radiohead’s “Street Spirit (Fade Out)” which is a huge love of mine and whether this was inspiration or not it’s definitely noticeable. This soon fades and we are back to descending into a new kind of misery and despair. Amongst the slow pounding drums and the crunchy guitars, it is the bass that comes full front and steals the show, again very reminiscent of my last review for Dysthymia. Throughout their tracks no matter how heavy or even light they were in places the bass always shone through like a beacon of warmth against the cold tremolo guitars and the same can be said here. The track’s pace elevates slightly at the midpoint and everything is dialled up to eleven, that is not before falling back gradually to a slightly quieter yet also emotional segment, the mixture of angelic vocals and bitter and scarred ones is back and in this brief yet small segment it works wonders. Soon though we are thrown back into a blast beat segment with intricate drum fills and raging guitars, however, this track ends on a much more sombre sound, just the guitars echoing off into the distance, no vocals no nothing. I do think stuff like that is very indicative of this genre, the sound of echoing chords or vocals means and shows what true emptiness sounds like, there is no distance between the guitar tones and the sound coming back, it just echoes out into the distance. Thus proving the vastness of just how lonely one person can be.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-08-23T19:28:59+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZPXMvEQKu/","text":"Track eight “Forgotten” really delivers on that grunge aesthetic it almost sounds like “Gallons of Rubbing Alcohol Flow Through The Strip” by Nirvana. The chords are very similar and slightly disjointed and her whispered angelic tones almost match those of Kurt Cobain’s muffled yet slightly whispered tones. This is only for a short period as the track soon opens up and engulfs you whole. Whilst this goes on another indie alternative act appears to mind with the guitar tone, here I feel a slight nod to that of Portishead and the track “Sour Times”. This conjures up some film noir vibes, whilst offering up a slightly spooky and forbidden taste. This once we approach the midsection does seem to fall by the wayside, and we enter into the much bleaker and darker territory once again. Here we now have elements of piano against guitar, sadly though this doesn’t last and we enter into a mire of sound, the rhythm guitars offer a constant grinding sound, almost offering up a wall of sound while the lead adds those familiar melancholic tones. It is the vocals in this closing part that do most of the heavier lifting of the track, from the harsh huskier tone that they usually have, here they enter into a more sickening tone, one where Haze almost sounds like she’s ready to throw up, this is very different, soon the huskier tones come back plus the angelic ones. Here this actually sounds like three spirits all in one room or three levels of personality all in one mind. Either way, it’s one hell of an ending and can put you right on the edge of your seat. As a second-to-last track, there is probably a reason that it’s taken such a dark dramatic turn.","img":[]},{"n":14,"date":"2026-08-23T19:31:19+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZPoTQEcJI/","text":"Track nine “Emptiness” starts with the sound of rain and thunder and I have to slide just one of the orange sponges off my skull to see if it’s actually raining here; lo and behold as I peer from behind a fallen column it certainly seems to be. Sliding the headphones back on I listen intently as the piano piece begins to start. Again like track four this appears to be a purely instrumental piece and one of a much more sombre nature than its predecessor. The element of the rain and thunder does help conjure up feelings of isolation and depression, however, I live to listen to music like this against the sound of rain. I actually find it quite soothing. Even on this track where it elevates in tempo after the halfway point, we still feel that element of loneliness in the track. As those notes slowly fade into the distance and we are left with just the sound of the rain and thunder and we wonder is this the music that accompanies our Funeral of Being?","img":[]},{"n":15,"date":"2026-08-23T19:33:06+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZP1YOEV6F/","text":"It would appear that my tape player has come to a stop with a loud click, slowly sliding the soggy sponge headphones off my skull, I reach into my bag and fumble for my tobacco, tin and lighter. Pulling out a single cigarette and lighting it from a little candle I inhale and embrace that sweet menthol taste. There is a lot to pack up on this release and I can imagine it’s the same for the individual who wrote it. I feel so much emotion has gone into recording this album and whilst these tracks have been deep, emotional, rabbit holes, with some incredible music behind it I feel like it could’ve benefitted from a little personal touch here and there. Yes, when you read the lyrics and listen to the music like I have, over and over again you can pick out some personal sections, however where I feel like this album would’ve benefited would’ve been maybe some of the titles and lyrics being in her native Ukrainian tongue. This I feel would’ve added a slightly more mysterious and darker twist to the album. That is, provided Haze does speak in her mother tongue. Tracks like Thoughts After The Gun Jams being “Думки після того, як заклинило зброю” or Ashes of my Former Self “Попіл мого колишнього” even so much as performing the songs in her native tongue may have just brought that little bit more edge or even mystery to the album. I understand the reason for wanting to do this in English as it helps to appeal to a wider audience but I do love that when you listen to acts like Shining perform their albums in Swedish it actually makes them sound really mysterious and even more sinister than they already are.","img":[]},{"n":16,"date":"2026-08-23T19:34:43+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcZQBLVkR_y/","text":"There is that slightly dangerous feeling of truly not knowing what you’re listening to until you translate it. Whilst I have little reservations about this release the music was indeed incredible and the vocals were just so agonising it made you really dial into the tracks just that little bit more. Do I feel that the tracks needed a few deeper more gruff style of vocals here and there, yes I do feel it could’ve benefited from it but overall this album is totally worthy and divine of the genre it is set in. With many single female DSBM acts popping up does My Last Promise have what it takes to stand out against the others? Yes, yes I do and maybe with the tweaks that I have noted this may either be food for thought or it may indeed be A Funeral of My Being. Packing up my possessions I look for a safe way out, I now assign myself as part of the bullet dodger squadron. Leaving my trademark black candle and ornate F emblazoned on a fallen column I slink off into the shadows once more. Until the bell tolls eight once more your humble servant in darkness. #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-08","artist":"Hypnotic Dirge Release","title":"I Live With What You Created","genre":"Atmospheric Black Metal / DSBM","date":"2026-08-16","cover":"img/album-08/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-08-16T18:48:33+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHJK5aEYw2/","text":"1. I Live With What You Created. 10:24\n2. We Can Never Go Back. 08:12\n3. To Choose Life is to Choose Grief. 04:07\n4. Until I Was Nothing. 10:10\n5. The Morning Darkness. 09:00\nTotal Running Time:\n\nAs another Sunday looms ahead, I’m staring at my calendar wondering just what review I have up this weekend. Floating over to my slope I sift through the mountains of correspondence from labels and bands trying to make sense of it all. Flicking through little scraps of parchment it’s clear to see that I need assistance in keeping track of what I have coming up next, after finding what I needed I realise that I need much more coffee and way more cigarettes. So, my first port of call before I even think about doing much else is making a fresh pot and whilst that happens I roll countless cigarettes. Everything prepped, I head to my hollows entrance where the sun proceeds to engulf the little ledge, once again dear old Blighty is suffering another week of unbearable temperatures and to cap it off the poor flesh tubes that inhabit the ground below me cannot even turn on a tap without the water police battering down their door, so this is making people even more miserable than they already are in this hell hole of a town. Sitting at the edge watching the smoke billowing from yet another wildfire somewhere in the distance, I despair at how close our little part of dear old Blighty is becoming like the US. Sipping my coffee and lighting my cigarette from the rays of the sun I yearn for the colder months to come, the first leaves falling from the trees, the sideways rain, bitter frosts and the morning darkness. Which, coincidentally, lines up with this Sunday’s review. Yes, that’s right on the scrap of parchment I have within my rotting hands are the words “Morning Darkness. A date scribed above it.","img":["img/album-08/p01-1.webp","img/album-08/p01-2.webp","img/album-08/p01-3.webp","img/album-08/p01-4.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-08-16T18:52:15+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHJmFwEbLr/","text":"Yes, that brings me to my announcement that my label bosses Hypnotic Dirge have asked me to pen my musings on a band that I have been very fond of since first hearing them, known as Dysthymia and their new album that is to be dropping titled “Morning Darkness.”\nIf you aren’t already in the know regarding this act, Dysthymia are a two-piece act hailing from Boston, Massachusetts also nicknamed Beantown hey if you know you know right? \n\tThis act has been active since 2021 bringing their own take on Depressive Black Metal, however, this isn’t your typical DBM yes they’re lyrical leanings to inner struggles and emotions of course but one of the driving factors behind this act also is their dedication to Anti-fascism, so much so that they fly under the banner of Red Nebula, this is a record label built on Antifascist Extreme Metal and I am one for it. With this little bit of knowledge imparted to my fellow readers, I place the parchment into one of the many makeshift pockets on my robes, finish my coffee, and put my cigarette out into the little bucket of sand I have outside my hollow, as I will not be a cause of these wildfires, no sir. Scowling back at the burning ball of gas in the sky and the dried dead world that lies underneath it I return to my slope.","img":["img/album-08/p02-4.webp"]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-08-16T18:54:44+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHJ4ODEWC4/","text":"Now, usually I would pack up and ship out to the location where the act hails from, however, with temperatures being what they are out there, plus the ever-growing threat of more wildfires, hands up to those in dear old Blighty whose phones didn’t sound like a World War Two air raid siren when getting alerted about this the other day? Yes, if I had a bowel I would’ve quite easily shit myself over how loud and brash it was. Nonetheless, this wasn’t to be as being as high up as I am I can see across the land and where these little incidents are taking place. Also id have no company as my feathery friend will not entertain flying out into these hellish temperatures and for that I don’t blame him, so we will sit in the darkened corners of my hollow crafting this review, candles lit, incense wafting out into the thick summer breeze and comfortable in the knowledge that the emergency services are doing their best to stave off the flames. \nRolling out a fresh piece of parchment, brimming my inkwell and sharpening my quill, I extract the files my label boss has sent me and transpose them to cassette, with all of this done I get ready to slide my new orange sponge headphones over my skull and sink deep into this new release and hope soon the Morning Darkness will descend back on us all.","img":[]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-08-16T19:01:49+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHKsFEEQ5S/","text":"Track one “I Live With What You Created.” Starts with what sounds like heavy rain against a window, long how I wish to hear that sound once more I press the orange sponge against my skull and sink deep into my chair. The faint sound of piano keys graces the left headphone which then bleeds through to the right, rolling thunder and the sound of traffic can be heard, it’s like we are sat in a car as the rain pours down. This is really setting the atmosphere for what is to come. All of a sudden without warning the guitars enter the tranquil sound of the rain, sounding sharp, yet not your typical chilling black metal sound, they collide together creating quite a dry and suffocating atmospheric sound much like what beholds anyone leaving their home in this sweltering 35 degrees Celsius heat, that’s 95 to all my American readers out there. The drums soon enter and thankfully it’s nothing too full-on, a nice slow repeating beat, one thing to note is the use of the cymbal as this sounds like rain splashing up from the ground and ties in extremely well with the intro. Soon agonising vocals enter the picture plus some well-timed orchestrated tremolo guitar tones. Again nothing too icy or cold, these are at just the right tone to sit above the rhythm section. The manner in which it’s all played together causes the track to elevate in tempo, nothing too fast if anything just a slight shift but noticeable to a discerning listener. One thing to note while all this is going on is the bass, beneath all that is going on in the foreground the bass has a walking bass line and this whilst in the background really shines through at peak moments.","img":[]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-08-16T19:03:23+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHK3jAkTcD/","text":"The vocals are really something to behold and are performed by Grushenka Ødegård the pain and anguish resonates through my skull but also matches the raw unbridled sound of the guitars stunningly. In the second or maybe third verse the tone drops a fraction, we lose the tremolo tone and the lead guitar and we see a hammer-on tone used, whilst this does replace the tremolo sound, the tone in which the hammer-on is used matches that of the previous tremolo. Here the vocals do most of the lifting in this segment which is truly justified as they are a real integral part of the track. Moving into the next verse the music takes on that uplifting and scaling sound, whilst still retaining that wonderful DBM sound, another noteworthy addition is the sound of clean vocals, I love that they’re slightly obscured by the guitars, but upon focusing hard you can indeed hear them. Coming back just as quickly as they faded the raw and husky brutal vocals return draping this track back in anguish and pain. Here the track truly takes back that really raw element sliding between the tremolo segment and the slow hammer-on effortlessly, yet still retaining that stunning DBM tone. Another thing to note is the level of overdrive used on the guitars, this adds a seductive level of fuzz to the rhythm section so when the little glimmers of solo come in from the lead they really pierce through along with any high screams and shrills from the front lady Grushenka. Moving into the last few verses of the track the use of the climbing scale on the guitar becomes more and more apparent and really helps build atmosphere. Between this and a little light jazz, it truly makes for such a dark and brooding vibe. The track slowly closes on the same tremolo-drenched sound as the opening, followed by a beautiful fade into the distance.","img":[]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-08-16T19:05:11+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHLEs9kf6e/","text":"Track two “We Can Never Go Back” opens with a beautiful slow guitar piece that when the lead joins just becomes something else, this is a piece you could imagine being written by any good artist but the fact that Dysthymia have come up with it makes it just even better. Whether it’s the tuning in which it’s played, or just how it’s been composed it just resonates with an entity like myself. Whilst this could be a structure created by any other artist I feel that the way Ray King has orchestrated, chosen the tuning and timing of the piece he’s still made it sound light, but still melancholic as well. That is before it lunges into the darker and droning chords that follow. This once again married up with the agonising vocals just adds to the thick, choking atmosphere. After this section has passed we spiral into a section where everything is amped up in tempo, blast beats, double kicks thundering bass lines and suffocating guitars, but what gets me again is the use of clean vocals amongst it, I couldn’t quite place my finger on just what these vocals reminded me of previous but on this track I hugged the headphones to my skull and truly honed in on their sound and they reminded me of a kind of grunge era sound particularly lead front woman of L7 Donita Sparks, or even Babes in Toyland Kat Bjelland. Either of these could’ve been an inspiration. However, if not I love the difference between these clean yet still grunge-inspired vocals to that of the bitter and harrowing screams. This is such diversity but well placed within the track. Another notable moment about this segment is the stunning guitar solo that takes place,this just truly shows Ray King at his finest.","img":[]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-08-16T19:06:31+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHLOfsEUKh/","text":"We soon fall back into the slow and agonising DBM section once again and despite its raw energy the bass segment of the track truly is sublime, the chords aren’t warming in any way if anything they display a level of hollowness to the track, of course I mean this in the best way possible. It’s also clear to see that the use of vocal stacking has been used as both soft angelic voices can be heard alongside the painful anguished screams. This is repeated over again in a following verse rounding out the track perfectly along with that stunning bass sound once more, before ending on a truly harrowing bass outro.","img":[]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-08-16T19:08:48+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHLfRfkY1N/","text":"rack three “To Choose Life is to Choose Grief” starts with your more typical black metal opening, and if anything this is the signature black metal track so far. This is not just signified by the tone of the instruments but also by the production, here the production level appears to have dropped significantly compared to that of the previous tracks. This however is not a diss because with how cold and stripped back this track is, it actually feels and sounds of the time. The guitars are pitch-perfect, cold, bleak and chilling, the drumming is much more in the background with the vocals, the bass is surprisingly in the midrange and once again stealing the show with its stunning rumbling notes. The guitars are at the front, making for that classic black metal sound we all know and love and this coupled with those shrieks and harsh throaty screams just makes for a truly demonic sound. In between these moments of sheer black metal wonderment are these truly rhythmic breakdowns, almost verging on tribal or primitive in nature, this doesn’t go amiss as the whole of this track is primal in every sense of the word. It comes with primal rage and primal rhythms making this quite the contrasting-sounding track but it does indeed work and work well may I add. I think it’s what this track needs, I do love my black metal and I love the ferocity of it, the cold and just how bleak it can feel but one thing that enhances this track is the little breakdowns we get and we do get treated to so many too, they’re slotted in two or three times over and this I feel breaks up the track beautifully, whilst simultaneously holding it together.","img":[]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-08-16T19:10:28+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHLradkd2G/","text":"The track is a short one and from what I can tell is the shortest track on the album. That doesn’t mean it doesn’t hit just as hard as the prior two, another thing to note is just how well the title matches the sheer bleak sound of the track, you can really see why they chose this hark back to the early nineties DSBM or even black metal sound. As we enter into the final outro of the track the tremolo sound is just like that of the intro piercing and icy once more and as the notes ring out the rhythm guitars fade into the distance leaving the track in sheer glassy silence.","img":[]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-08-16T19:12:14+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHL4dLEcp6/","text":"Track four “Until I Was Nothing” opens slowly and once again with some beautifully drawn-out guitar work, but those clean vocals when they hit ohhh my days. Call them angelic, melancholic, call them what you will they’re stunning and they add to the track’s ambient intro so much. This is so contrasting from the previous track both musically and vocally but this is what I’m talking about regarding Dysthymia, they’re so different to most DBM acts, they can touch on sounds and vocal qualities rarely used. This act is not afraid of anything least of all mixing it up. I can hand on blackened heart say I could listen to this intro over and over again not only that if the whole track had consisted of this kind of vibe I still would’ve been down for it. This however wasn’t to last, as soon as we are thrust right back into the classic black metal sound we’ve all come to know and love. This is very much in a similar vein to that of the previous track, the production is a little lower, but like the last track previous it works so well with the tonality of this side of the coin. Between the heavy tremolo segments and blast beats there are slower moments in the more raw segment, here the guitars are draped in both overdrive and distortion creating this raw yet slow and agonising segment. What helps this much like previous tracks is the mixture of both angelic vocals and the more brutal harsh nature. In these raw and unbridled moments it has to be said the bass does really shine through, if anything on this album the bass could be seen as the highlight of the album, it keeps and works well with the drums in all the best ways possible, it highlights everything in its rawest form, I will say is the only place it truly gets a little lost and that is when the clean vocals enter, especially when they enter the more raw and harsh moments, however, that’s most likely down to the multilayering of the clean vocals, I guess they just drown the bass out. Nonetheless, the bass is there when it’s counted and where it’s needed.","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-08-16T19:13:30+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHMBsLkWJ0/","text":"This is indeed a lengthy track and quite possibly the longest one on here at a whopping 10:10. So as we approach the end of this track together there is a segment where the clean vocals and guitar tones took me back to 1994 and a particular and prominent movie came out, not only that it had a soundtrack to die for, that movie was indeed “The Crow” and the track that struck me sounded a little like the outro to this track it was none other than Medicine and the track “Time Baby II” the mix of discordant guitars and that heavy distortion on top plus those soft female vocals from Annette Zilinskas, yes it took me straight there, this is the power that good music can have. The final segment of the track leaves you with a spoken-word segment from frontwoman Grushenka, this truly stays with you as the final guitar chords echo out.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-08-16T19:15:17+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHMOvUEZCO/","text":"Track five “The Morning Darkness” and title of our album starts with some stunning jangly almost indie inspired guitar work, you could almost be forgiven for thinking this was gonna be a jovial happier piece, then you realise your listening to Dysthymia and you realise there is going to be shift somewhere, however that doesn’t mean they can’t lure you into a false sense of security because even though this reaches a happy scale and there are elements of clean vocals almost alluding to more joyful times ahead. It all soon changes, whilst we do keep the clean vocals for a verse or two, it doesn’t indeed shift back to the more anguished and sorrowful-sounding ones and when the subject matter is what it is with this act it’s easy to see why. This however, isn’t a stab at the act they have an incredible sound and the ability that Grushenka has the ability to use both clean and harsh vocals together creates quite balance much like the lighter and darker aspects of the music. I will say though that since making a tie to Dysthymia sounding a little like that of the band Medicine, I can’t actually get that out of my head and this track echoes similar tones heard in the track Time Baby II. In the lowest and most discordant parts, the guitars sound truly incredible, both audible and crisp.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-08-16T19:16:45+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHMZeyEQCg/","text":"The layering, the overdrive and the fuzz used as effects just emphasise everything and once again the bass is still the secret star of the show, those notes that beautifully waltz between the wall of guitar sounds truly do something incredible. This track truly cuts deep in every sense of the word, it’s both bleak, cold and truly emotional, nothing emphasises that more than listening to the harrowing cries from Grushenka. As the track starts to drift into its final moments little light speckles appear to try and eclipse the darkness of the raw black metal, however, this does nothing to take away from the final tears and spoken word of this incredible track as the emotions pour out your left with a feeling of isolation, despair and hurt inside of you, if anything the work of Dysthymia is done and what a job they have done this track truly leaves you feeling like your alone in the Morning Darkness.","img":[]},{"n":14,"date":"2026-08-16T19:18:34+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHMmzBEQV1/","text":"As I slide the orange sponge headphones from my skull, fumble for my cigarettes and place one where my tooth once resided. Lighting it I inhale feel and try to take stock of all I’ve listened to. Whilst this has only been 5 tracks the length and depth that this album has gone to is nothing short of impressive to say the least. However, despite the genre it sits in I have found it more accustomed to influences from the grunge era. Yes, it’s had moments of ferociousness and those signature tropes of black metal like the harsh vocals, tremolo guitars and the occasional blast beat. However, I cannot escape the clean vocal segments that have resonated with me so profoundly. These moments have led me to side more with female-fronted grunge bands like L7, Babes in Toyland and the mighty Medicine. These bands carved a niche in the grunge world yet seem to get forgotten about by the likes of Alice in Chains Nirvana and Soundgarden, which in turn could say the same about this act Dysthymia. Whilst they are within the region of DBM or DSBM you don’t hear as much love for them as they deserve. Bands like Forgotten Tomb, Lifelover, Shining, and Bethlehem all steal the spotlight, when Dysthymia has just as much to offer as the other aforementioned acts. I’m a firm believer in paying it forward when it comes to inspiration and with many female-fronted DSBM bands or projects coming up, not even they seem to tip their blackened veils to this act.","img":[]},{"n":15,"date":"2026-08-16T19:20:31+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcHM1FDESfk/","text":"I’ve loved this act since first stumbling across them on their first self-titled full-length. What can certainly be said is that between this release and The Morning Darkness, a lot of maturing and focus has been done to create an album so driven, so sonically absorbing as this. Here lie five tracks all ranging from over the ten-minute mark so don’t feel you’re not going to get your money’s worth here because you sure as shit will, however, you won’t see certain tropes that have found their way into DSBM over the years. If anything I love what Dysthymia have done here, whether intentional or unintentional the grunge influence is there and this for me separates this wheat from the chaff of the other bands. A new breed of DBM has risen and much the same as the title it’s ready to drape us in “The Morning Darkness.” your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-09","artist":"Necromaster","title":"A Lone Warrior","genre":"Dungeon Synth","date":"2026-08-09","cover":"img/album-09/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-08-09T17:16:36+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db09FXvDJ6r/","text":"In under two hours, the bells will toll eight and a new deep dive review will be going live. That's right at 8:00 pm, my musings of @necromasteruk “A Lone Warrior” will be up for all to read. So, come down to my hollow where candles burn and incense meets the stench of this dried dust ball we call home. Your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":["img/album-09/p01-1.webp","img/album-09/p01-2.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-08-09T18:59:06+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1I0DKEe-0/","text":"1. A Lone Warrior Part I. 05:07\n2. A Lone Warrior Part II. 05:16\n3. A Lone Warrior Part III. 09:15\n4. A Lone Warrior Part IV. 06:08\nTotal Running Time:\n\nAfter taking a short two-week break, I have finally returned. In this time I have celebrated my 450th year on this rock and have been haunting some of my nearest and dearest with my presence and not in a malicious way may I add. Sometimes you need to take time to check on those that hold that little segment deep in the darkest recesses of my blackened heart. So, with no time like the present, I am indeed back to deliver you more in-depth knowledge and musings on releases that make their way to me, and in this case, it comes in the form of Necromaster and his latest release, “A Lone Warrior.” Released in April of this year I realise I may be a little late to the party for this release, however, it is the entity himself that reached out to me through the void and asked if I would indeed take a listen to his latest offering. So, on another truly scorching day, I sit, robes dangling down from my hollow fulfilling my morning ritual of countless coffees and cigarettes when my new telephonic device gives out a shrill. Rustling in my robes I take out my phone and lo and behold the entity known as Necromaster has indeed messaged asking if I would take listen to his latest offering that is set to be distributed by one of my label bosses Dale of Shadows. Now, never one to shun work from this incredible label who’s given me so much fodder over the recent years to sink what remaining teeth I have into I happily accepted the offering. We strike up conversation and in between chit-chat, Necromaster slides me some download codes for not just this release but three more. This wets one’s appetite greatly, not only that I then learn that the entity and I were practically neighbours at one point, yes that’s right before I set up camp in my hollow I reside in now, I inhabited a section of dear old Blighty known as the North East of England, immediately this led me to look at my map that adorns my hollows wall and sure enough he was indeed talking about the same place I once reside.","img":["img/album-09/p02-2.webp"]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-08-09T19:00:33+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1I-q6kX58/","text":"However, our paths changed and here we are in different places still discussing the rural and stunning features of the North East. It truly puts a perspective on just how small the world truly is and with that I rush over to my slope where I, quickly dub the files to a blank cassette and load it into my shiny new Walkman that now has new orange sponge headphones, May I add and I get ready loading my bag with all the accoutrements one needs, I even look back at my feathery fiend who’s nestled in a tight corner of the cave I let him know it’s only a short flight and that he really should join me, Hell it’ll be cooler where we’re travelling to than here. So with that, he hops onto my robes and we take off into the blistering sun-drenched sky back to my hometown of Gainsborough. While the entity from this act doesn’t actually live in this section of the North East, I cannot pass up the chance of visiting my home town and getting some inspiration. Just a short trip back up north I get ready to land in my home town and boy is the landscape around it changing, it’s a sleepy town with not much going on in it, but the one thing that’s taking over is more and more houses, wherever a patch of farmland or field once stood there are now hundreds upon hundreds of houses being built upon it. This does indeed sadden me as this once rural and lush green landscape has now been taken over by brick dwellings that don’t exactly fit the aesthetic of where they have been placed. Sadly this is the way of the world now. Landing slap bang in the market square the cobbles do nothing for a soft landing. Even this section of the town has changed, now sporting a posh cinema complex with new shops and eateries, you can really see that they’re trying to bring this forgotten town centre back to life.","img":[]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-08-09T19:03:01+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1JQyMkTHM/","text":"Whilst most shops remain empty sadly and the mix of more modern buildings has started to squeeze in between others that date from Tudor times. Also, there is the Old Hall which is the greatest site of a Tudor building you could imagine and on top of that you have All Saints Church which I at one point in my life I resided in a house across the road from, as the memories flood back and a faint beat restarts in my heart, I know I have to escape this town centre. I head straight for the Old Hall, it would appear that they’re just about to close up for the day and before they shut the thick timber doors on the world outside I sneak in undetected. Lying low in hidden recesses of the building I watch the staff vacate and seal me into this crumbling yet still opulent building. I enter what appears to be the banquet hall, thick solid wood tables like the walls that are still all held together with wattle and daub. Thick terracotta tiles line the floor but it’s the vaulted high ceilings made with thick timber that are the real focal point. I pull the bench in at the wooden table, unfurl my parchment and place the candles and incense on the table. The sun is beginning to set and as the sun slowly extinguishes from the bright blue sky the darkness sets in, this provides just the ambience I need to pen this review. So without further a-due I present to you the works of Necromaster and “A Lone Warrior.”","img":[]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-08-09T19:06:53+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1JtCSkaFQ/","text":"Track one “A Lone Warrior Part I.” Opens with som deep echoing chords on the synth,which when greeted by lighter notes almost match the setting that I am sat in. Starting slow and maybe even cumbersome in their pace this truly shapes the opening of the album. Soon a beat starts to gather pace below the thick chords and lighter synth notes elevating the piece from that slow lumbering start to a more running free pace. It is here that the story of our lone warrior is to unfold and this is aided by the throaty whispers that flow through the notes telling us the tale of the intrepid warrior. Sadly with no lyrics to find, it doesn’t make it hard to identify just what is being uttered by our dear entity Necromaster. Nonetheless, when pressing the headphones tight to my skull, some words can indeed be made out and it is indeed words about our Lone Warrior. Whilst production is a little low on the scale of this release, I feel it doesn’t detract from the overall atmosphere of the piece. If anything I think it enhances it. I do find it refreshing to have something so wonderfully composed on the synth, with that hazy atmosphere layered over the top, it adds an aged effect like this could’ve been recorded in the early nineties, or late eighties as part of a truly detailed video game soundtrack or even movie soundtrack. I say this because other acts that have tried this sometimes try just a little too hard and it feels forced and overproduced. Here, though this has been done with love and patience and maybe even with a decent amount of appreciation of what came before. I love how the notes and chords have a faint waver to them as the cassette ribbon has slightly stretched over time from constant or high use of playing. This truly again adds to the overall feel that this recording could be from that era. Another thing to note is the vocals, they aren’t way up in the foreground distracting you from the music, they’re right in the middle, this allows the notes of the synth and the effects to truly consume you and pull you into the atmosphere being created.","img":[]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-08-09T19:09:02+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1J80ZES5L/","text":"The vocals do remind me of another entity known as Moorgeist whom I have been getting into quite recently, his vocals work is very similar and if this was indeed an inspiration then what an artist to choose because his works are of very similar calibre. The track here has many wonderful high points throughout where they almost touch on an ethereal/ heroic level. However, there still resides that sweet melancholic undertone that stops it running away with itself. This is a stunning opening track and I hope the rest of the album follows a similar vein as this. This has been well crafted, it pays homage to older eras beautifully and I feel strangely drawn in, plus playing in the venue I sit in this is truly very befitting.","img":[]},{"n":7,"date":"2026-08-09T19:10:39+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1KIqcERkg/","text":"Track two “A Lone Warrior Part II.” Starts with a similar opening as that of the first track, however, this seems much more sombre in tone. This is amplified further as the intro moves from just the synth to the introduction of either Flute, clarinet, and maybe Oboe. These are generated I assume through the synth, and over the melancholic chords that play below them they seem to add a little light to this quite dark track. This track you could imagine being used whilst rolling the dice on a D&D board game to add ambience. The track has a very journey feel to it, like if one were to wander through the woods alone. I think what aids this is indeed the wind instruments, this adds a touch of what it would be like if birds were to be in the trees above whilst you trudged through the dense forest. When hearing this you can imagine the higher notes representing the spring, autumn or winter sun piercing through the treetops. There are no vocals upon this track and this is how I feel this track should’ve truly been done. This is strictly an instrumental piece and what a piece it is. Something to note also is that whilst this is merely a fraction longer than that of the opening track it does indeed seem shorter, which is ironic in many ways as obviously it’s not. Nonetheless, this track has all the tropes of late eighties and early nineties dungeon synth or dark ambient. I do feel that on this piece there are some elements plucked from the Nordic scene, particularly Mortiis and Burzum especially the early years and despite what you think or want to say about the latter of those two acts, the entity behind that project created a niche over night sensation known as Dungeon Synth and before you all get on your high horses and shout it was Mortiis first blah, blah, you do realise that “Jim Kirkwood” was penning stuff in the early nineties way before Mortiis had left Emperor. Anyway, I digress, still these pioneers created a genre and it’s great to feel this get recognition still to this day and influencing acts of today. Hence once again why the approach to this type of track has been done with the utmost respect and appreciation.","img":["img/album-09/p07-1.webp","img/album-09/p07-2.webp"]},{"n":8,"date":"2026-08-09T19:12:50+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1KYqUEVsR/","text":"Track three “A Lone Warrior Part III” opens with a slight beat that is repeated to more whispering vocals once more and as soon as it starts it draws you deep into the atmosphere. Much the same as the opening track it has that old worn effect added on top to give it that really honest and old feeling. The chords are deep and undulating while the higher-pitched notes give a slight Phantom of the Opera feeling to the track. The whispering vocals shift to that of a spoken word, still with some slight distortion to them they become hard to pick up in places, however, I feel this adds to the overall ambience of it. As we reach the midsection we get a beautiful instrumental section, that delves into the flute segment like that of the track prior. Here though this is a much more sublime and dreamlike scene of the music. No dark tones grace this piece and this midsection becomes quite the light ambient section of the track. Again no lyrics or spoken word can be heard so this tranquil moment can be appreciated for all it is worth. Little splashes of cymbals enter into this moment and soon enough the track starts to build once more, as we head back into the dark tones of the opening. It’s almost like if this was to appear in a fantasy film or RPG that our Lone Warrior was indeed on a journey, one that starts in a dark realm and the more he moves throughout this realm he comes to a clearing where light touches everything around him, yet one step too far and he will once again find himself back in the darkness once more where the whispering and rasping tones call him once more. There is. A subtle battle between light and dark on this album and it works well against one another.","img":[]},{"n":9,"date":"2026-08-09T19:15:45+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1KuBSEea-/","text":"There isn’t a great divide if anything it’s subtle and well orchestrated. This has made for very well-balanced tracks and whilst there is a short number of tracks upon this release they’re packed full of adventure and more. This track is the longest of all the tracks upon the album, clocking in at nearly ten minutes, however, it doesn’t give off the feeling of being lengthy. I put this down to the separation or as stated before the balance between light and dark tones on the track being such that it leaves you feeling balanced. Either way, it’s yet another pleasurable piece to listen to that doesn’t drag on or leave you feeling like When is this going to end. If anything each of the tracks on this release has left me feeling like I want more, not just from the album but from Necromaster himself.","img":["img/album-09/p09-2.webp"]},{"n":10,"date":"2026-08-09T19:18:33+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1LCizEfdG/","text":"Track four “A Lone Warrior Part IV” starts with long, drawn-out dark chords which capture me straight away, when the lighter tones come in beneath they don’t represent your common synth tones, here you have the synth mimicking the likes of violins, cellos and bass. This brings a much richer and deeper tone to this opening and one that I love deeply. As the track builds slightly in places I do love the slight melancholic feel it leaves you with a soft comforting feel. As I stopped the cassette and rewound it multiple times I found myself honing in on its sound, and I felt it reminded me of something. Pausing I roll a cigarette and light it from one of my candles when it hits me, this opening is very reminiscent of “Air On G String” by Johann Sebastian Bach. Now, if you’re old as fuck like me you’ll remember this from the old Hamlet Cigar Commercials, and as soon as I heard it and lit my cigarette the grey matter swirled around my skull and the visions flashed before me. Still, if this was a minor inspiration for this piece, what a piece of classical music to draw inspiration from. Possibly one of the most well-renowned classical pieces composed and brought to the forefront by another talented artist. Now, I’m not saying this has been directly lifted in any way, but it is possibly an inspiration. This is another track where there are no vocals and this is just an instrumental piece, once again the notion of balance plays a huge part in the album’s overall sound with two tracks holding vocals and two not. The balance between the feeling of sadness and joy upon the tracks is something else’s that’s worthy of note.","img":[]},{"n":11,"date":"2026-08-09T19:19:34+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1LKCNEfK7/","text":"I guess you could say that this album has quite the harmonious feel to it, it touches on everything and as I gaze up at this wonderful structure I sit in I start to marvel at its creation and very existence still to this day. While the notes fill my skull you do start to get a strange feeling this is the end chapter for Necromaster at this stage, well until his next release I hope. The track plays out on more heavenly-sounding flute and stringed segments almost like our Lone Warrior is being carried off into the afterlife, the track doesn’t come to an abrupt stop much like all the tracks before you get the last sounds of old school tape hiss which is just another added element to making this music nostalgic as that of music from the day.","img":[]},{"n":12,"date":"2026-08-09T19:21:39+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1LZTHkekc/","text":"Sadly the cassette comes to a stop and here in this great hall I slowly slide the headphones off, roll a cigarette and light it from the drying embers of my candle. Not only do I take stock of what I have listened to but also the majestic building that surrounds me. Whilst this has indeed been quite the short album only consisting of four tracks this album has indeed been full to the brim with every emotion and feeling imaginable, which has led to old wounds opening and made me ponder deeply about aspects of my life and just how I got to where I am. I too can relate to this album and its tones, as I feel like a lone warrior somewhat myself. I’ve always carried things deep within, sometimes never truly letting go of scenarios and chances won or lost. I’m not one to celebrate my victories nor am I one to mourn my losses. I take stock of it and push it deep down hoping it will one day go away. This is something I picked up on regarding the compositions on this album, this album truly gets the feeling of light and dark, loss and joy, the equilibrium of the album is two halves and neither one can be celebrated as it’s just about one lone warrior and it’s these feelings and sounds that transmit through the music in the most profound way possible. This may be a short album but it’s packed full of absolutely everything one person could need even the balance between Dungeon Synth and Black Metal is perfect. This now makes me want to head back and check the entity’s earlier works because whether or not they link together in sound or style, from this album a concept could be put together for this individual known as the Lone Warrior in future albums.","img":[]},{"n":13,"date":"2026-08-09T19:24:12+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/Db1Lr9bEVl6/","text":"This is only my idea, however, should the entity behind the project like it then he may indeed use it. I guess at the end of the day we are all our own warrior, we carry battle scars, burdens, grief, joy and happiness in different ways, it’s just how we channel it that’s important and finally I feel I’ve found the perfect vessel through that of Necromaster. \nRising, the sun shines through and it’s time to leave, I hear the iron keys turning in those dense locks. Scooping up my gear I throw it into my bag, my faithful feathery fiend hops onto my robes and before they can discover me I leave my trademark F engraved in the table along with the remnants of a black candle. Until the bell tolls eight next week one and all, I am your humble servant in darkness. #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":[]}]},{"id":"album-10","artist":"Dusklurker / Hypnotic Dirge","title":"Celestial Fantasies & Wild Unboxings","genre":"Black Metal & Raw Ambient","date":"2026-08-14","cover":"img/album-10/p01-1.webp","posts":[{"n":1,"date":"2026-08-14T17:08:17+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcB0G3jjIvI/","text":"#blackmetalmondayunboxing on a Friday? Came in multiple drops so it is with honour I present to you @tardigradaofficial first full length “Emotionale Ōdnis” which roughly translates to “Emotional Wasteland, Barrenness or Desolation. Issued on three formats digital download, digipack CD or Cassette through the mighty @eternityrec each cassette is hand numbered. Id like to thank a good brother @gravfrost.synth for turning me on to this act, this album does not disappoint and is heightened with true atmosphere. My darkest regards go out to the three entity’s behind the project and to all whom issued this incredible piece. Your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":["img/album-10/p01-1.webp","img/album-10/p01-2.webp","img/album-10/p01-3.webp","img/album-10/p01-4.webp","img/album-10/p01-5.webp"]},{"n":2,"date":"2026-08-14T18:04:54+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcB6lhOjHji/","text":"#blackmetalmondayunboxing on a Friday pt.2. Yessir indeed it was and yet another offering from the mighty @tardigradaofficial their second full length titled “Vom Bruch bis zur Freiheit”  which translates to “From fracture to freedom.” Once again, I’d like to thank my brother in darkness @gravfrost.synth for turning me on to this mighty trio of entity’s whom have made yet another stoic atmospheric black metal album. This was once again issued through @eternityrec and each cassette was hand numbered to a limited quantity. My darkest regards to all three entity’s whom reside on this album and to the labels that issued it. Your humble servant in darkness #foras  #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":["img/album-10/p02-1.webp","img/album-10/p02-2.webp","img/album-10/p02-3.webp","img/album-10/p02-4.webp","img/album-10/p02-5.webp"]},{"n":3,"date":"2026-08-14T20:11:44+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcCJGdtkTtm/","text":"#blackmetalmondayunboxing on a Friday? Yessir this is both a caught in the wild & huge collection builder for the entity known as #moorgeist and the self titled E.P officially released March 31st 2020 as an independent digital download. On the same date two limited edition cassette tapes were released through worship tapes. A whole year later @poisonous_sorcery_official would issue this very vinyl that came complete with black CD of the E.P. incense in a black envelope, patch and poster. Sadly no exact date can be given on this release date. Finally on November 7th 2021 @dodrecords would release their own 12” vinyl. Having slept on this entity previously, it came as a complete surprise when I finally found this. A great score at a superb price May I add. So here in all its dark black metal glory is none other than Moorgeist. To both the entity behind this project and the labels that issued it I thank you. Your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":["img/album-10/p03-1.webp","img/album-10/p03-2.webp","img/album-10/p03-3.webp","img/album-10/p03-4.webp","img/album-10/p03-5.webp"]},{"n":4,"date":"2026-08-14T20:42:03+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcCMkgHkcJ6/","text":"#blackmetalmondayunboxing on a Friday? Yessir this is both a caught in the wild & huge collection builder for the entity known as #moorgeist and here in all its blackened majesty is the entity’s second E.P titled “Hymnen Der Nacht.” Which translates to “Hymns Of The Night.” Once again this piece of evil majesty was birthed as an independent digital download on May 30th 2020 quite the quick succession after the entity’s first release. On the same date it would be Worship Tapes that would issue two limited edition cassettes. It wouldn’t be till the end of the year, November 15th 2020 that @dodrecords would release this version on 12” vinyl. Then in April of last year @dodrecords would once again release another 12” vinyl repress this time in two different colours. Once again my darkest regards to both the entity Moorgeist and all involved with this bleak black release. Your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":["img/album-10/p04-1.webp","img/album-10/p04-2.webp","img/album-10/p04-3.webp","img/album-10/p04-4.webp"]},{"n":5,"date":"2026-08-14T21:09:46+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcCPveLEX31/","text":"#blackmetalmondayunboxing on a Friday? Yessir this is both a caught in the wild & huge collection builder for the entity known as #moorgeist and here in all its glory is the Split Release between the entity himself Moorgeist and Hergandrom titled “Nightwind Spells.” Officially released as an independent digital download  November 11th 2020 and like time and memorial it would once again be Worship Tapes that would release the second incantation on limited edition cassette on the same date. On 5th of August a year later @dodrecords would issue a limited edition digipack CD and digital download. However this extremely rare purple vinyl version doesn’t seem to be noted anywhere. This stunning vinyl version is a proud edition to my collection and I’d like to extend my darkest regards to the entity Moorgeist and all that issued this exquisite release. Your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":["img/album-10/p05-1.webp","img/album-10/p05-2.webp","img/album-10/p05-3.webp","img/album-10/p05-4.webp"]},{"n":6,"date":"2026-08-14T21:36:49+00:00","url":"https://www.instagram.com/p/DcCS1l4EZ03/","text":"#blackmetalmondayunboxing on a Friday? Yessir this is both a caught in the wild & huge collection builder for the entity known as #moorgeist and this was the final item in the collection. Here in all its glory is the first full length by the mysterious entity Moorgeist titled “Helcaraxë.” Issued first November 7th 2021 as once again a digital download, it wouldn’t receive a physical release for a mere 10 days through @moosundefeu as a limited edition cassette. December 5th of the same year 2 x 12” vinyls in a gatefold sleeve would be released through of course @dodrecords which is what you see before you. However, mine came with the added bonuses of being autographed by the Entity Moorgeist himself. So this was indeed the best surprise. Lastly August 5th 2022 @dodrecords would issue a digipack CD variant. As always I send my darkest regards to both Moorgeist and all labels that brought this release to life. I shall forever be your humble servant in darkness #foras #lordoftheshadows🕯️💀⛓️⚔️🥀","img":["img/album-10/p06-1.webp","img/album-10/p06-2.webp","img/album-10/p06-3.webp","img/album-10/p06-4.webp","img/album-10/p06-5.webp","img/album-10/p06-6.webp","img/album-10/p06-7.webp"]}]}];
